\section{CONCLUSION}
This paper presents a recursive solution of moving horizon estimation for nonlinear time-variant systems. We further establish the conditions under which MHE and EKF are equivalent, regardless of the horizon size. The unification between MHE and FGO is also clarified. All three methods are implemented and compared for GNSS localization.

% We find that appropriately handling the arrival cost and tuning the horizon size can improve the localization performance of MHE. Considering that eliminating measurement errors is challenging \cite{wen2021factor}, a context-aware MHE with a learnable arrival cost is a promising research direction \cite{zhang2024gnss}.

Our results show that carefully managing the arrival cost and tuning the horizon size can enhance the localization performance of MHE. Given the difficulty of eliminating measurement errors \cite{10506762,10706359}, a context-aware MHE with a learnable arrival cost is a promising research direction \cite{zhang2024gnss}.










