% In today's era of rapid technological advancement and increasing reliance on computational systems, the efficient scheduling of multi-task jobs in multiprocessor environment has become critically important. This problem, known as multiprocessor multitask scheduling (MPMS), is essential for optimizing the performance and scalability of applications across various domains, for example, in the fields of cloud computing and machine learning. In this paper we study MPMS problem in both deterministic and stochastic models, where each job have several tasks and is complete only when all its tasks are finished. We propose $\mathsf{NP}$-$\mathsf{SRPT}$ algorithm, which is proved to achieve a competitive ratio of $\ln \alpha+\beta+1$ for minimizing response time, where $\alpha$ denotes the ratio between maximum job workload and minimum job workload, $\beta$ represents the ratio between maximum non-preemptive task workload and minimum job workload. In addition, the competitive ratio achieved is shown to be order optimal, when there are constant number of machines. We further consider the problem under Poisson arrival and general workload distribution (\ie, M/G/N system), and show that $\mathsf{NP}$-$\mathsf{SRPT}$ achieves asymptotic optimal mean response time when the traffic intensity $\rho$ approaches $1$, if task size distribution has finite support. Beyond finite task workload, the asymptotic optimality of $\mathsf{NP}$-$\mathsf{SRPT}$ also holds for infinite task size distributions with certain mild probabilistic assumptions, including M/M/N system. We have also conducted experimental validations to confirm the asymptotic optimality.
