\subsubsection{Preset-Based Asset Generation}
% While CharFaceGAN enables expression generation through semantic mask manipulation, a fully automated pipeline must automatically manipulate the semantic mask. 
While CharFaceGAN enables expression generation through semantic mask manipulation, a fully automated pipeline requires automatic mask manipulation.
Therefore, we develop a library of preset template shapes representing standard facial expressions and visemes (mouth shapes corresponding to phonemes). These presets, defined by semantic labels, can replace a figure's original expression (Fig.~\ref{fig:face_exp_gen_results}, top row).

Our automatic expression generation pipeline is illustrated in Fig~\ref{fig:preset_gen}. Given a figure's face image and its semantic masks from CharSegNet, we first identify salient points along the contours of the mouth and eye regions. These points, marking top, bottom, left, and right boundaries, establish correspondence with predefined points on our preset shapes. These correspondences enable Thin-Plate Spline deformation~\cite{24792} to transform preset shapes from their canonical form to match the facial feature's position and proportions. The deformed preset labels then replace the original eye and mouth regions in the semantic mask, which guides CharFaceGAN in generating new facial features that maintain the character's style.

While quite close, the features generated by CharFaceGAN may not perfectly align with the semantic boundaries. To address this, we refine the semantic masks to better adhere to the generated face using the approach proposed in \citet{DBLP:journals/corr/abs-2005-02551}.
% We then inpaint the original image's eye and mouth regions using LAMA~\cite{suvorov2021resolution}, chosen for its speed and reliability in avoiding unwanted feature hallucination. 
We inpaint the eye and mouth regions using LAMA~\cite{suvorov2021resolution}, which avoids unwanted feature hallucination while being fast.
The refined generated features are composited onto the inpainted image to produce the final result. As shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:face_exp_gen_results}, this pipeline can automatically generate various facial expressions by combining different eye and mouth presets while preserving the original artistic style.

\subsubsection{Audio-Driven Animation}
Building on our preset-based asset generation, we enable automatic facial animation synchronized to audio input. For lip synchronization, we first convert the audio to text and use the Montreal Forced Aligner~\cite{MFA} to obtain time-aligned phonemes. These phonemes map directly to our viseme presets, allowing us to select and composite the appropriate mouth shapes in sync with the speech. Eye animations can be controlled through various methods to specify when different expressions should appear, whether for basic actions like blinking or for conveying emotion. The result is a complete facial animation pipeline that brings hand-drawn figures to life while maintaining their original artistic style.
%\textbf{\underline{Automatic Preset-based Generation:}}Though CharFaceGAN can generate novel facial assets via manual manipulation of semantic mask, we need an automated approach for facial asset generation. To this end, we propose to leverage predefined set of abstract shapes/presets which can represent different facial expressions across all characters. For mouth animation, we propose to use standard abstract viseme-shapes as per the ARPABET format, a standard practice in traditional animation pipelines. As shown in \autoref{fig:preset_library}, we design these presets as semantic labels (mainly for eyes and mouth) which can be use to replace the default expression of the character. \autoref{fig:preset_gen} illustrates the proposed fully-automated pipeline for preset-based novel facial expression generation for hand-drawn characters. Given the image of a character's face and its semantic face mask predicted using CharSegNet, we first estimate salient points - top, bottom, left, right points for the semantic contours of mouth and eye regions. The presets also have predefined salient points associated with them. These salient points are used to perform correspondence-guided Thin-plate-spline(TPS)-based deformation of the presets from canonical space to character space. We remove the original eye and mouth labels and place merge the deformed preset labels appropriately to produce the modified semantic segmentation mask. This mask is fed to CharFaceGAN with the original image as style reference to reconstruct the image with modified facial features. Since, we are only interested the newly generated eye and mouth regions, we extract them out using semantic segmentation labels. Please note that the generated image might not respect the exact semantic boundaries, therefore we refine the semantic masks using a mask refinement approach proposed in \cite{CascadePSP??}. To inpaint the original mouth and eyes from the input image, we employ inpainting method proposed in \cite{LAMA} which is extremely fast, and tends to produce hallucination-free results. The extracted assets are then composited on top of the inpainted image to produce novel facial expressions. Using the proposed approach, we can automatically generate different combinations of eye and mouth presets, yielding a diverse range of facial expressions as shown in \autoref{fig:face_exp_gen_results}.\\
