\begin{abstract}
The proliferation of intelligent applications at the wireless edge, alongside the exponential growth of multimodal data, poses challenges for deploying multimodal large models (MLMs) in resource-constrained networks. These constraints manifest as limited bandwidth, computational capacity, and stringent latency requirements, particularly under low signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) conditions. To overcome these limitations, we propose a token communication paradigm that facilitates the decentralized deployment of MLMs across user devices and edge infrastructure (e.g., base stations). In this paradigm, task-relevant tokens are extracted from multimodal inputs and serve as the primary medium for communication between distributed model components.
%
To align semantics and optimize transmission efficiency, we propose a dual-pronged approach:
1) We design a contrastive split fine-tuning method to project heterogeneous modalities into a shared feature space, enabling seamless interaction between model components while preserving modal-specific semantics.
2) We employ a lightweight compression technique to reduce the size of transmitted tokens, minimizing bandwidth consumption without sacrificing task-critical information.
The proposed framework integrates collaborative fine-tuning of both the foundation model and multimodal transceivers, ensuring that token generation and utilization are tailored to specific downstream tasks.
%
Simulation experiments conducted under different SNR conditions demonstrate that our method results in a $13.7\%$ improvement in test accuracy. Furthermore, our approach exhibits quicker convergence rates, even with reduced token lengths, highlighting the promise of token communication for facilitating more scalable and resilient MLM implementations in practical multiuser networks.
\end{abstract}

\begin{IEEEkeywords}
	Token communications, multimodal large models, cross-modal alignment, resource-constrained networks.
\end{IEEEkeywords}

\section{Introduction}
The thriving of artificial intelligence (AI) and wireless communication has ushered in an era of edge intelligence \cite{edge-intelligence}. Wireless devices, such as smartphones, drones, and Internet
of-Things (IoT) sensors, generate vast amounts of multimodal data that hold immense potential for training and refining AI models. These heterogeneous data streams hold tremendous potential for training and refining AI models, laying the foundation for intelligent applications in diverse domains such as autonomous systems, personalized healthcare, and smart cities. Effectively harnessing this wealth of multimodal data is crucial for unlocking the full potential of AI and driving the next generation of intelligent services.

Multimodal large models (MLMs) aim at amalgamating various modalities such as image,
audio, and text, into a unified framework, unlocking the potential for handling diverse data types \cite{survey}. These models excel at extracting cross-modal correlations, enabling tasks such as visual question answering \cite{llava}, scene understanding \cite{scene}, and contextual reasoning \cite{deepseek}. The generalization capabilities of MLMs stem from pre-training on large-scale datasets, which allows them to adapt to a wide range of downstream tasks with minimal fine-tuning. However, despite their impressive capabilities, deploying such models at the edge remains a significant challenge. The immense size of MLMs requires substantial computational and memory resources, rendering them impractical for real-time processing on resource-constrained devices. Furthermore, uploading raw multimodal data to centralized servers for remote fine-tuning introduces prohibitive communication overhead, particularly in bandwidth-limited wireless environments. This dilemma highlights the need for a tailored solution that balances computational efficiency with communication constraints in distributed systems.

To mitigate the computational burden of deploying MLMs at the edge, split learning offers a practical solution by partitioning the model between devices and edge servers \cite{wanli,huiqing}. However, transmitting high-dimensional intermediate features still incurs substantial communication overhead.
To further address this issue, semantic communications have been explored as a promising approach that shifts the focus from traditional bit-level transmission to semantic-aware compression, aiming to reduce data redundancy and improve communication efficiency \cite{semantic}. By leveraging neural network-based encoders to extract and transmit high-dimensional semantic representations, semantic communications make it possible to deploy multimodal large models at the network edge. However, current frameworks in semantic communications mainly focus on data reconstruction at the receiver, overlooking the requirements of task-oriented fine-tuning. Moreover, how to effectively incorporate cross-modal contextual information into semantic communication systems remains largely unexplored. To this end, token communications have been proposed as a novel paradigm that treats tokens as the fundamental transmission units, enabling multimodal language models to better capture both token semantics and inter-token relationships, i.e., context, for more effective input processing \cite{tokcom1}.

In this paper, we propose a token communication paradigm tailored for decentralized MLM deployment across user devices and the base station (BS). Specifically, we design a contrastive split fine-tuning method to align multimodal representations into a shared feature space. To enhance robustness against wireless channel noise, we incorporate a textual reconstruction loss during model training. Additionally, we employ a sliding window-based token compression method to preserve temporal dependencies while reducing sequence length, which minimizes communication overhead. Following this, we fine-tune the foundation model to boost feature extraction, thereby enabling the efficient transmission of task-oriented tokens.
\begin{itemize}
	\item
	We propose a cross-modal alignment strategy tailored for token communication in multimodal scenarios. By treating text as the anchor modality, we unify heterogeneous representations (e.g., text, audio, vision) into a shared feature space using a combination of inter-modal contrastive loss and textual token reconstruction loss. This approach not only enhances inter-modal consistency but also improves robustness against wireless channel noise, ensuring reliable feature alignment under realistic communication constraints.
	\item 
	To enable efficient feature transmission, we design a task-oriented split fine-tuning method that jointly optimizes the transceivers and the server-side foundation model.  This method transforms high-dimensional multimodal features into compact task-relevant tokens, reducing communication overhead while preserving critical semantic information.  By focusing on task-specific information during fine-tuning, the model learns to prioritize features that directly contribute to task performance, thereby facilitating streamlined and efficient token transmission.
	\item
	Simulation results show that the proposed alignment strategy accelerates fine-tuning convergence and significantly boosts model accuracy under varying signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) conditions.  While less tokens may initially reduce accuracy due to information loss, their lower dimensionality leads to faster convergence.  Consequently, a moderate reduction in token length achieves a trade-off between communication efficiency, convergence rate, and task accuracy.
\end{itemize}

