\section{System Model}

\begin{figure*}
	\centering
	\includegraphics[scale=0.21]{SysModel.png}
	\caption{Task-oriented token communications in resource-constrained multiuser networks.}
	\vspace{-3mm}
	\label{fig:SYS}
\end{figure*}

As illustrated in Fig.~\ref{fig:SYS}, we aim to deploy and fine-tune MLM in a multi-user wireless network consisting of $M$ devices and a BS equipped with an edge server.
%
The network processes data from a set of $M$ modalities, denoted as $\mathcal{M} = \{1, 2, \ldots, M-1, T\}$, where each element in $\mathcal{M}$ corresponds to a distinct data modality. Specifically, $T$ represents the text modality, while the remaining elements $\{1, 2,\ldots, M-1\}$ represent non-text modalities (e.g., audio, vision, etc.).
%
To mitigate the computational and storage burdens of MLMs, as well as the communication expenses incurred by directly uploading raw data, we strategically deploy token transmitters on devices. Meanwhile, token receivers and the foundation model are deployed on the BS and edge server, respectively. The token transmitters efficiently extract task-specific features from multimodal raw inputs and transform them into token representations, which are subsequently transmitted over the wireless channel.
%
Specifically, each device transmits only one modality of dataset $\mathcal{D}_m = \{\bm{x}_{m,l}\}^{D_m}_{l=1}$, where $D_m = \vert \mathcal{D}_m \vert$ denotes the total number of samples of modality $m \in \mathcal{M}$. We consider the $l$-th data from different modalities share the same semantic meaning, i.e., for the $l$-th semantic concept, we have data pairs $\mathcal{D}_l = \{\bm{x}_{T,l}\} \cup \{ \bm{x}_{m,l} \}^{M-1}_{m=1}$ across different devices. 

During each training round, we sample a mini-batch $\mathcal{B}_m$ from the dataset $\mathcal{D}_m$ on each device. 
To achieve the cross-modal fine-tuning \cite{Align-then-refine}, we divide the MLM fine-tuning process into two stages: cross-modal alignment and task-oriented fine-tuning.


\subsection{Cross-Modal Alignment}
As the majority of foundation models are pre-trained on textual data, the first stage of our scheme is dedicated to aligning non-text modalities with the textual feature space  through the training of token transceivers.
\subsubsection{Token transmitter}
For textual data $\bm{x}_T$, the text encoder $\mathcal{E}_T$ parameterized by $\Theta^E_T$ can be expressed as
\begin{align}\label{yt}
	\bm{Y}_{T} = \mathcal{E}_{T} (\bm{x}_{T}; \Theta^E_T),
\end{align}
where $\bm{Y}_{T}\in \mathbb{R}^{b \times s_{T} \times d_{T}}$, where $b$ is the batchsize, $s_{T}$ is the sequence length, $d_{T}$ is text feature dimension.

For data $\bm{x}_{m}$ of modality $m \in \mathcal{M}$, $ m \neq T$, the corresponding modality encoder $\mathcal{E}_{m}$ parameterized by $\Theta^E_m$ can be expressed as
\begin{align}\label{em}
	\bm{E}_{m} = \mathcal{E}_{m} (\bm{x}_{m}; \Theta^E_m), \quad \forall m \in \mathcal{M}, m \neq T,
\end{align}
where $\bm{E}_{m} \in \mathbb{R}^{b \times \hat{s}_{m} \times d_{m}}$, with $\hat{s}_{m}$ denoting the sequence length and $d_{m}$ the feature dimension for modality $m$.
Due to the fact that non-textual tokens often exhibit extremely long sequence lengths (e.g., $\hat{s}_m=3137$ for video tokens encoded by ViViT \cite{vivit} and $\hat{s}_m=1214$ for audio tokens encoded by AST \cite{ast}), it is challenging to transmit them directly over wireless channels. To preserve temporal dependencies while reducing sequence length, we simply apply a sliding window to select a subset of $s_m$ tokens from the original sequence, which can be expressed as
\begin{align}\label{bar_em}
	\bar{\bm{E}}_{m} = \mathrm{Slide} (\bm{E}_m), \quad \forall m \in \mathcal{M}, m \neq T,
\end{align} 
where $\bar{\bm{E}}m \in \mathbb{R}^{b \times s_{m} \times d{m}}$ and $\mathrm{Slide}(\cdot)$ denotes the sliding window operation that extracts $s_{m}$ tokens evenly from $\bm{E}_{m}$.
To align the feature dimensions with the input of the foundation model, we further project $\bar{\bm{E}}_m$ into the same feature dimension $d_T$ as the text tokens via a fully connected layer $\mathcal{F}_{m}$ parameterized by $\Theta^F_m$, which can be expressed as
\begin{align}\label{ym}
	\bm{Y}_{m} = \mathcal{F}_{m} (\bar{\bm{E}}_{m}; \Theta^F_m), \quad \forall m \in \mathcal{M}, m \neq T,
\end{align}
where $\bm{Y}_{m} \in \mathbb{R}^{b \times s_{m} \times d_T}$. $\bm{Y}_m$ are then reshaped into complex-valued signals for transmission over the wireless channel \cite{tmsc}.
\begin{align}
	\bm{y}_m=\mathrm{reshape}(\bm{Y}_m), \forall m \in \mathcal{M},
\end{align}
where $\bm{y}_m \in \mathbb{C}^{b\times\frac{s_md_T}{2}}$.


\subsubsection{Token receiver}
