The token receiver preprocesses the received multimodal signals to obtain channel-robust cross-modal aligned tokens as the input to the foundation model. Specifically, the received signal $\tilde{\boldsymbol{y}}_m$ can be expressed as
\begin{align}
	\tilde{\boldsymbol{y}}_m=h_m \boldsymbol{y}_m+\boldsymbol{n}_m, \forall m \in \mathcal{M},
\end{align}
where $\tilde{\bm{y}}_m \in \mathbb{C}^{b\times\frac{s_md_T}{2}}$, $h_m$ is the channel coefficient and $\bm{n}_m$ is the IID channel noise vector which follows symmetric complex Gaussian distribution $\mathcal{CN}(0,\sigma^2\boldsymbol{I})$ with zero mean and variance $\sigma^2$.
Finally, the received signal is reshaped back into the token space
\begin{align}
	\tilde{\bm{Y}}_m=\mathrm{reshape}^{-1}(\tilde{\bm{y}}_m), \forall m \in \mathcal{M},
\end{align}
where $\tilde{\bm{Y}}_{m} \in \mathbb{R}^{b \times s_{m} \times d_T}$.
We use an MLP projection head $\mathcal{G}_m$ parameterized by $\Theta^G_m$ as the neural part of token receiver, which consists of two fully-connected layers with a ReLU activation in the middle, and such structure is proven useful in improving the quality of representations \cite{fedcl},
\begin{align}\label{y'm}
	\bm{Y'}_m=\mathcal{G}_m (\tilde{\bm{Y}}_m;\Theta^G_m),
	\quad
	\forall m \in \mathcal{M},
\end{align}
where $\bm{Y'}_{m} \in \mathbb{R}^{b \times s_{m} \times d_T}$. 
These projections are applied in parallel, enabling simultaneous transformation across all modalities without parameter sharing.
We treat text as the anchor modality. Thus, we introduce a reconstruction loss on the textual tokens $\bm{Y'}_T$ to combat channel noise, which can be expressed as
\begin{align}\label{rec}
	\mathcal{L}^{\rm Rec}_{T} = \Vert \bm{Y'}_T-\bm{Y}_T \Vert^2.
\end{align}
Furthermore, we introduce the contrastive loss to achieve cross-modal feature alignment.
Since tokens for different modality have different sequence length, i.e., $s_{m} \neq s_{m'}$ for $m, m' \in \mathcal{M}$ and $m \neq m'$, it is not feasible to directly quantify the differences between tokens across modalities. Thus, we use a pooling operation to extract the features $\bm{a}_m$ of each modality
\begin{align}\label{am}
	\bm{a}_m = \mathrm{Pooling}(\bm{Y'}_{m}),
	\quad
	\forall m \in \mathcal{M},
\end{align}
where $\bm{a}_m \in \mathbb{R}^{b \times d_T}$. 
Letting $\text{sim}(\boldsymbol{u},\boldsymbol{v})=\boldsymbol{u}^\top\boldsymbol{v}/\|\boldsymbol{u}\|\|\boldsymbol{v}\|$ denotes the cosine similarity, we align the multimodal features $\bm{a}_{m}$, $\forall m \in \mathcal{M}$, $m \neq T$ with textual features $\bm{a}_{T}$. The contrastive loss for the modality $m$ can be expressed as
\begin{align}\label{con}
	\mathcal{L}^{\rm Con}_{m}=-\log\frac{\exp(\text{sim}(\bm{a}_{m},\bm{a}_{T})/\tau)}{\sum_{k=1}^{K}\exp(\text{sim}(\bm{a}_{m},\bm{a}_{T})/\tau)},\forall m \!\in\! \mathcal{M}, m \!\neq\! T,
\end{align}
where $\tau$ denotes a temperature parameter, a larger $\tau$ leads to less sharp distinctions between positive and negative pairs. Since the contrastive loss constrains the pooled features $\bm{a}_m$ rather than directly constraining the multimodal tokens $\bm{Y'}_m$, it achieves cross-modal alignment while maintaining token-level details, thus preserving the necessary modality-specific characteristics.
We use the reconstruction loss \eqref{rec} to optimize $\mathcal{G}_T$, and contrastive loss \eqref{con} to optimize $\mathcal{F}_m$ and $\mathcal{G}_m$, $\forall m \in \mathcal{M}$, $m \neq T$ till converge and then integrate them with foundation model for task-oriented fine-tuning.


