\subsection{Task-Oriented Fine-Tuning}
After completing the cross-modal alignment stage, we concatenate the outputs of the token receivers $\bm{Y'}_{m}$ from different modalities as the input to the foundation model. Through concatenation, we simplify the input structure of the model, avoiding multiple inferences of the same semantic information. At the same time, the concatenation operation provides richer feature information for the same semantics, which helps enhance the model's ability to understand complex scenarios. Specifically, in the $n$-the training round, $n \in \{1, 2,\ldots, N\}$, the concatenation process can be represented as
\begin{align}\label{concat}
	\hat{\boldsymbol{Y}}_n=\mathrm{Concat}(\boldsymbol{Y}_{1,n}^{\prime},\boldsymbol{Y}_{2,n}^{\prime},\ldots,\boldsymbol{Y}_{M-1,n}^{\prime}, \bm{Y}^{\prime}_{T,n}),
\end{align}
where $\hat{\bm{Y}}_n \in \mathbb{R}^{b\times S_n \times d_T}$ and $S_n=\sum_{m\in\mathcal{M}}s_{m,n}$. Then $\hat{\bm{Y}}_n$ is forwarded to the foundation model deployed on the edge server.
We consider the LoRA adapter for parameter-efficient fine-tuning \cite{lora}. For the foundation model $\bm{W}_0 \in \mathbb{R}^{d_T \times h}$ with frozen parameters, we training its low-rank adapter $\Delta \bm{W}_n = \bm{B}_n\bm{A}^\top_n$, where $\bm{A}_n \in \mathbb{R}^{h \times r}$, $\bm{B}_n \in \mathbb{R}^{d_T \times r}$ and $\mathrm{min} (d_T, h) \gg r$. The fine-tuned foundation model can be given by
\begin{align}
	\bm{W}_n = \bm{W}_0 + \Delta \bm{W}_n = \bm{W}_0 + \bm{B}_n\bm{A}^\top_n.
\end{align}
The low-rank adapter $\Delta \bm{W}_n$ with frozen foundation model $\bm{W}_0$ is updated to minimize the following loss function for task $\mathcal{R}$:
\begin{align}
	\mathcal{L}^{\rm Task}_n = \frac{1}{b} \sum_{l \in \mathcal{Y}} L_n(\Delta \bm{W}_n;\bm{W}_0,\hat{\boldsymbol{Y}}_{n,l}, \mathcal{R}),
\end{align}
where the token set $\mathcal{Y} = \{\hat{\boldsymbol{Y}}_{n,l}\}^b_{l=1}$, $\hat{\boldsymbol{Y}}_{n,l} \in \mathbb{R}^{1 \times S \times d_T}$ is the $l$-th token and $L_n(\Delta \bm{W}_n;\bm{W}_0,\hat{\boldsymbol{Y}}_{n,l}, \mathcal{R})$ is the loss function associated with token $\hat{\boldsymbol{Y}}_{n,l}$ under the frozen foundation model $\bm{W}_0$.

The forward propagation process for token $\hat{\boldsymbol{Y}}_{n,l}$ can be expressed as $\bm{Z}_n=\hat{\boldsymbol{Y}}_{n,l}(\boldsymbol{W}_0+\boldsymbol{B}_n\boldsymbol{A}_n^\top)$.
The gradients of low-rank adapter matrices $\bm{A}_n$ and $\bm{B}_n$ are given by
\begin{align}
	\bm{G}^{\bm{A}}_n = \frac{1}{b} \sum_{l \in \mathcal{Y}} \nabla_{\bm{A}_n} L(\Delta \bm{W}_n;\bm{W}_0,\hat{\boldsymbol{Y}}_{n,l}, \mathcal{R}), \nonumber \\
	\bm{G}^{\bm{B}}_n = \frac{1}{b} \sum_{l \in \mathcal{Y}} \nabla_{\bm{B}_n} L(\Delta \bm{W}_n;\bm{W}_0,\hat{\boldsymbol{Y}}_{n,l}, \mathcal{R}),
\end{align}
Thus, the update of the low-rank adapter can be given by
\begin{align}
	\Delta \bm{W}_{n+1} &= \bm{B}_{n+1} \bm{A}_{n+1}^\top \nonumber \\
	&= (\bm{B}_n - \eta \bm{G}^{\bm{B}}_n)  (\bm{A}_n - \eta \bm{G}^{\bm{A}}_n)^\top,
\end{align}
where $\eta$ is the learning rate. For token transceivers, we keep the parameters $\Theta^E_{m,n}$ of encoder $\mathcal{E}_m$ frozen since they has been pre-trained on large-scale datasets and possesses powerful feature extraction capabilities.
Specifically, the gradients of the token transceivers $\Theta^G_{m,n}$ and $\Theta^F_{m,n}$ can be given by
\begin{align}
	\bm{G}^{\Theta^G}_{m,n} = \frac{1}{b} \sum_{l \in \mathcal{Y}} \nabla_{\Theta^G_{m,n}} L(\Delta \bm{W}_n;\bm{W}_0,\hat{\boldsymbol{Y}}_{n,l}, \mathcal{R}), \nonumber \\
	\bm{G}^{\Theta^F}_{m,n} = \frac{1}{b} \sum_{l \in \mathcal{Y}} \nabla_{\Theta^F_{m,n}} L(\Delta \bm{W}_n;\bm{W}_0,\hat{\boldsymbol{Y}}_{n,l}, \mathcal{R}).
\end{align}
Thus, $\Theta^G_{m,n}$ and $\Theta^F_{m,n}$ can be updated as
\begin{align}
	\Theta^G_{m,n+1} = \Theta^G_{m,n} \!-\! \eta \bm{G}^{\Theta^G}_{m,n},
	\Theta^F_{m,n+1} = \Theta^F_{m,n} \!-\! \eta \bm{G}^{\Theta^F}_{m,n}.
\end{align}
For the text modality $m = T$, the input tokens are already in the same dimension as the foundation model. Therefore, the projection module $\Theta^F_{T}$ is not required. In other words, the optimization of $\Theta^F_m$ is only applied to modalities other than text, while the optimization of $\Theta^G_m$ is applied to all modalities, including text.

