In the simulations, we consider a multimodal communication network involving text, audio, and visual modalities. The foundation model adopted is Qwen2.5-1.5B \cite{qwen} with its low-rank adapter $r=4$, which is deployed on the edge server. For modality-specific encoders, we utilize the text encoder from the Qwen model for textual inputs, the AST \cite{ast} for audio inputs, and the ViViT \cite{vivit} for visual inputs, all of which are deployed on edge devices. The SNR between devices and the BS is set to 10 dB. The contrastive temperature $\tau=0.07$.

We first perform cross-modal alignment on the VALOR-32K dataset \cite{valor}. VALOR-32K is a human-annotated tri-modal dataset originally designed for the audio-visual captioning task. It contains diverse audio events, including both human speech and various environmental sounds, providing rich multimodal information for learning effective cross-modal representations.
After the alignment stage, we fine-tuning the MLM on audio-visual question answering (AVQA) task using the MUSIC-AVQA dataset \cite{music}. Unlike other audio-involved datasets where the audio is primarily human speech, which typically aligns more naturally with textual information, both VALOR-32K and MUSIC-AVQA contain more diverse and complex audio characteristics. Furthermore, unlike previous works that adopt a discriminative paradigm \cite{music}, we evaluate the autoregressive generation capability of the foundation model. Specifically, the output space of our model spans the entire vocabulary instead of a limited set of label indices, which significantly increases the task difficulty, as it demands a deeper understanding of the multimodal context and a higher level of semantic reasoning.

