%When $\tau$ is set to $0.07$, the model achieves the fastest convergence rate, along with a final accuracy comparable to that with $\tau=0.13$. In contrast, when $\tau$ is reduced to $0.03$, Excessive alignment suppresses modality-specific features, thereby hindering performance and leading to slower convergence the unaligned baseline.



Fig.~\ref{length} illustrates the impact of different compression rates for audio and visual tokens length on model performance.
In the unaligned setting, both the audio and visual token lengths are set to $s_A=128$ and $s_V=128$. Under the same token length, the aligned version exhibits significantly faster convergence.
Moreover, appropriately compressing the token length can lead to better performance. Specifically, although excessive compression (e.g., setting $s_A$ and $s_V$ to 16) results in substantial information loss and degraded performance, the best results are achieved when the audio token length $s_A=64$ and visual token length $s_V=32$, rather than the longest length of $s_A=128$ and $s_V=128$.
Reducing the number of tokens significantly decreases the input dimensionality for the fusion backbone, leading to faster convergence rate during training.

Fig.~\ref{snr} shows the model performance under different SNR. Compared to the unaligned version with $s_A=128$ and $s_V=128$, the cross-modal aligned model achieves a minimum improvement of $7.3\%$ in test accuracy at SNR = $0$ dB, and a maximum improvement of $13.7\%$ at SNR = $6$ dB. However, unlike the results shown in Fig.~\ref{length}, shorter token lengths do not yield higher test accuracy, despite converging faster. Although fewer tokens shorten the optimization trajectory, they tend to reach suboptimal solutions. Nevertheless, after modality alignment, the test accuracy achieved with shorter tokens still surpasses that of the unaligned version with longer tokens. Moreover, despite using a smaller ($1.5$B) foundation model that is pretrained solely on text, our aligned and fine-tuned framework achieves even higher performance ($47.9\%$) than much larger multimodal pretrained models, e.g., $47.6\%$ for OneLLM-$7$B \cite{onellm} and $43.0\%$ for ChatBridge-$13$B \cite{cb}.

