\section{Conclusion}
In this paper, we proposed a task-oriented token communication paradigm for efficient information exchange among distributed model components of MLM. By leveraging cross-modal contrastive split learning, non-text modalities were aligned into a unified feature space guided by textual representations, enabling seamless inter-modality interactions. The resulting cross-modal aligned tokens were compressed and concatenated into compact task-relevant sequences, significantly reducing communication overhead while preserving critical information. To enhance task-specific processing, a server-deployed foundation model was co-optimized with the multimodal token transceiver, ensuring effective extraction and utilization of transmitted tokens. Simulation results illustrated that cross-modal aligned token transceivers exhibited faster convergence and higher test accuracy in downstream tasks under varying SNR conditions. While a reduced number of tokens may lead to initial performance degradation due to decreased information content, the resulting lower input dimensionality led to faster convergence.
