\title{On Distributed Colouring of Hyperbolic Random Graphs}
\date{}

\begin{document}
\allsectionsfont{\sffamily}
\maketitle
\begin{abstract}
We analyse the performance of simple distributed colouring algorithms under the assumption that the underlying graph is a \emph{hyperbolic random graph} (HRG). The model of hyperbolic random graph encapsulates some algorithmic and structural properties that also emerge in many complex real-world networks like a power-law degree distribution. Following studies on algorithmic performances where the worst case is replaced by analysing the run time on a hyperbolic random graph, we investigate the number of rounds and the colour space required to colour a hyperbolic random graph in the distributed setting.

First, we show that the simplest randomised algorithm terminates after exactly 2 rounds \aas when the colour space is a constant fraction of the maximum degree $\Delta$ and the input graph is a hyperbolic random graph. Moreover, we show that even with substantially more colours than the chromatic number, the algortihm never terminates \whp We then study two further variants of randomised distributed colouring algorithms that are more tailored towards hyperbolic random graphs. For a randomised algorithm with ID priority, we show tight bounds up to a multiplicative polylogarithmic factor for the colour space required: for any constant $\eps>0$, the algorithm produces a $\eps\cdot\Delta$ colouring in two rounds with probability $1-\bigO(n^{-c})$ for a constant $c>0$, while for a colour space $\Delta^{1-\eps}$ the algorithm never terminates \whp The final randomised algorithm we study gives priority to higher degree vertices. For this algorithm we show that on a hyperbolic random graph it produces a colouring within two rounds \aas with a colour space that is $\Delta^{1-\delta}$ for some constant $\delta > 0$. Moreover, for a certain regime of the power law exponent of the hyperbolic random graph, we show that this colour space for the algiorithm is optimal up to polylogarithmic factors. All three randomised variants are applicable in the CONGEST model.

Finally, we show a deterministic colouring algorithm for the LOCAL model which colours a hyperbolic random graph in constant rounds with a colour space that is close to that of the chromatic number. 


\end{abstract}
