\section{Introduction}

In this paper, we study the problem of distributed vertex colouring on real-world networks modeled as Hyperbolic Random Graphs (HRGs). The goal of the vertex colouring problem is to assign a colour to each vertex such that adjacent vertices receive different colours.

We consider this problem in the classic LOCAL model, where each node represents a computational entity that communicates with its neighbours in synchronous rounds. At the end of the algorithm, each node is required to output its own part of the solution—for example, its assigned colour. The time complexity of an algorithm in this model is measured by the number of synchronous communication rounds required until all nodes have produced their outputs.
Over the decades, algorithms have improved from $\bigO(\log n)$ to sublogarithmic and even $\poly\log\log n$ rounds, though they remain technically complex—using graph decompositions, symmetry breaking, and reductions to MIS, which itself relies on deep derandomisation techniques. Interestingly, on HRGs, even these advanced algorithms can achieve $\bigO(\log^* n)$ rounds when colouring with sufficiently many colours, but they still depend on non-trivial coding-theoretic methods. This motivates our exploration of whether simpler algorithms can efficiently colour HRGs. While HRGs have $\bigO(\log n)$ diameter and can be trivially coloured with the optimal number of colours by having a designated leader node learn the whole graph topology, brute-forcing the solution locally, and re-distributing it to the nodes of the network, such an approach is highly unrealistic, abuses the unbounded message size of the LOCAL model, and offers no algorithmic insight\footnote{In fact, we show that one can in fact colour HRGs with  $(1+o(1))\chi(G)$ colours in $\bigO(1)$ rounds exploiting the structure of HRGs and exploiting  the unbounded message size of the LOCAL model similar to $\bigO(\text{diameter}) \in \bigO(\log n)$ round algorithm, and hence also providing little algorithmic insight.}. 


In this paper, we ask the question whether there are simple distributed algorithms to colour real-world graphs, optimally, faster and with significantly fewer colours than all much more complicated involved existing algorithms. 



We use the simplest randomised distributed colouring algorithm as our baseline approach:


\begin{tcolorbox}
\textbf{Random Colour Trial (RCT):}
Each node randomly picks a uniformly random candidate colour from its palette of available colours, i.e., the whole colour palette without the colours used by its already permanently coloured neighbours, and sends this colour to its neighbours while receiving their colours. If no neighbour tries to get coloured with the same candidate colour, the node permanently adopts the colour, otherwise it retries in the next round with a new random candidate colour from an updated palette. This process repeats until all nodes have a valid colour.
\end{tcolorbox}


It is simple, local, relies only on small messages, and---when using $\Delta + 1$ colours, where $\Delta$ is the maximum degree of the graph---is known to converge in $\bigO(\log n)$ rounds with high probability on any graph. As messages only contain colours, it also runs in the CONGEST model, where communication is restricted to $\bigO(\log n)$-bit messages per edge per round.

\subparagraph{Why $\Delta + 1$ colours?} Vertex colouring with $\Delta+1$ colours is the most studied colouring problem in  distributed computing and many other related modern models of computing like streaming, massively parallel computation, local computation algorithms. The reason lies in a classical result from graph theory: to guarantee that a proper colouring exists and that the colouring process always terminates, we typically use $\Delta + 1$ colours, where $\Delta$ is the maximum degree of the graph. This upper bound ensures that each node always has at least one available colour not used by its neighbours, regardless of how the colouring unfolds. In distributed settings, it is known that already colouring with a single colour less changes the entire nature of the problem with larger lower bounds on low-degree graphs, and even  more involved algorithms. In order to make fast progress in the distributed setting large parts of the network need to be coloured in parallel, but a careless partial colouring of the graph, may result in an uncoloured subgraph on which we can even existentially not complete the colouring. 


\subsection{Our Contribution.}
We show that the simple \RCT algorithm performs significantly better on real-world graphs. In fact, it colours them in a constant number of rounds with high probability and with significantly fewer colours than the best existing advanced distributed algorithms. 


In particular this very simple algorithm colours a hyperbolic random graph within 2 rounds \aas\footnote{See \Cref{item:prob-guarantees} for the definitions of probabilistic guarantees.} Hyperbolic random graphs (HRGs) are a generative graph model where vertices are thrown onto a hyperbolic disk and edges connect a pair of vertices if the pair is close in hyperbolic distance to each other. A power-law degree distribution emerges from this process and it is one of the typical properties of a HRG~\cite{gpp-rhg-12, pkbv-info-2010}. More formally, the  probability that a vertex has degree $k$ is given by $\sim k^{-(2\alpha + 1)}$ where the model parameter $\alpha\in (1/2,1)$ influences the probability distribution on the hyperbolic disc and thus indirectly  the power-law exponent. It turns out that many fundamental graph properties of HRGs like the maximum degree~\cite{gpp-rhg-12}, the spectral gap \cite{hrg-spectral}, the second largest component \cite{km-slcrhg-19}, the tree-width \cite{bfk-tw-2016} or the clique number and degeneracy \cite{bmrs-stacs-25} depend on $\alpha$. We call a graph $G$ a \emph{typical hyperbolic random graph} if $G$ fullfils all properties that hold \aas on a hyperbolic random graph. For \RCT, we show the following theorem:

\begin{restatable}[Random colour trial]{theorem}{mainTheorem}\label{thm:main-theorem}\text{ }
\begin{enumerate}
    \item For any constant $\eps>0$, \RCT executed with $\eps\cdot \Delta$ colours on a typical hyperbolic random graph with maximum degree $\Delta$ terminates after exactly 2 rounds \aas 
    \item There exists a constant $\delta >0$, such that \RCT executed with $n^{\delta}\cdot \chi$ colours on a typical hyperbolic random graph with chromatic number $\chi$ never terminates \wehp
\end{enumerate}
\end{restatable}

\smallskip


\Cref{thm:main-theorem} improves upon using the best existing algorithm for the problem in several ways. It is much simpler, it is faster, and it uses fewer colours. 


\smallskip

\textbf{How fast can we colour in general?}
Linial's seminal $\Omega(\log^* n)$ lower bound for deterministic distributed colouring with $\Delta+1$ colours was extended to randomised algorithms by Naor. These bounds are often cited as applying to general graphs—but in fact, they only hold on graphs with maximum degree two, i.e., collections of paths and rings. For general graphs, the best published lower bound is that $\Delta+1$ colouring (and even $\bigO(\Delta)$ colouring) cannot be solved in one round with a deterministic algorithm. Interestingly, for sufficiently large $\Delta$—at least some $\poly\log n$—there exist algorithms that achieve $\Delta+1$ colouring in $\bigO(\log^* n)$ rounds. Whether this is tight, or whether constant-round algorithms might suffice even for $\bigO(\Delta)$ colouring, remains one of the most fundamental open problems in distributed computing. While we do not claim that our result of \Cref{thm:main-theorem} extends to general graphs, it is nevertheless striking that on HRGs, the simplest randomised algorithm achieves proper colouring with few colours in exactly two rounds.






