The key property is that $v_{b_w}(g_{(u,w)})$ and $v_{b_w}(g_{(v,w)})$ are large enough so that in approximate equilibria, the buyer $b_w$ always wins the goods $g_{(u,w)}$ and $g_{(v,w)}$ and the payment on these two goods is $\alpha_{b_u}+\alpha_{b_v}$.

\paragraph{NPURIFY gates.}
Fix a $\NPURIFY$ gate $(u,v,w,\NPURIFY)$. 
We set the budget of $b_v$ to be $1.8$, and set the budget of $b_w$ to be $2.8$.

For the buyer $b_v$, we create an auxiliary buyer $c_v$ and create a good $g_v$ for them, namely, only $b_v$ and $c_v$ have non-zero values on the good $g_v$. Similarly, for the buyer $b_w$, we create an auxiliary buyer $c_w$ and create a good $g_w$ for them, namely, only $b_w$ and $c_w$ have non-zero values on the good $g_w$. In particular, we let  
