\paragraph{The complexity of the algorithm for weakly consistent trees.} Similar to the case for the strongly consistent trees, the subroutine that gives the sibling of a small tree for a fixed vertex $u$ takes $O(n^2)$ time. However, in the new algorithm, we only need to sample and test $O(\log{n})$ vertices $u$ for each iteration. Similarly, the root test and the tree merging subroutines both use $O(\log{n})$ vertices and $\tilde{O}(n^2)$ time. Furthermore, since we can roughly reduce the instance size by a $(1-1/\polylog{n})$ factor every iteration, the entire process converges in $\polylog{n}$ iterations. As such, we could argue that the total running time is $\tilde{O}(n^2)$, and the longest chain of dependent calls is $\polylog{n}$. This would imply a near-linear time offline algorithm and a parallel algorithm with near-linear work and poly-logarithmic depth.

