By writing the perturbed initial value as 
\begin{equation*}
\widetilde{y}_0=y_0+\varepsilon \Vert y_0\Vert \widehat{z}_0,
\end{equation*}
where $\widehat{z}_0\in\mathbb{C}^n$ is a unit vector (i.e. $\Vert \widehat{z%
}_0 \Vert=1$) indicating the direction of perturbation, we obtain 
\begin{equation}
\delta \left( t\right) =K\left( t,y_{0},\widehat{z}_{0}\right) \cdot  \label{magnification}
\varepsilon ,
\end{equation}%
where 
\begin{equation}
K\left( t,y_{0},\widehat{z}_{0}\right) :=\frac{\left\Vert \mathrm{e}^{tA}%
\widehat{z}_{0}\right\Vert }{\left\Vert \mathrm{e}^{tA}\widehat{y}%
_{0}\right\Vert }  \label{Ktayz0}
\end{equation}%
with $\widehat{y}_{0}:=\frac{y_{0}}{\left\Vert y_{0}\right\Vert }$ the \emph{normalized initial value}. We define $K(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0)$ as the \emph{%
directional condition number} of the problem (\ref{due}).

In general, we know nothing about the direction $\widehat{z}%
_{0}$ of the perturbation of $y_0$. Therefore, it is useful to introduce 
\begin{equation}
K\left( t,y_{0}\right) :=\max\limits_{\substack{ \widehat{z}_0\in \mathbb{C%
}^{n}  \\ \Vert \widehat{z}_0\Vert =1}}K\left( t,y_{0},\widehat{z}%
_0\right) =\frac{\left\Vert \mathrm{e}^{tA}\right\Vert }{\left\Vert \mathrm{e%
}^{tA}\widehat{y}_{0}\right\Vert },  \label{KAy0}
\end{equation}%
where $\left\Vert \mathrm{e}^{tA}\right\Vert $ is the matrix norm of $%
\mathrm{e}^{tA}$ induced by the vector norm $\left\Vert \ \cdot \
\right\Vert $. We define $K(t,y_0) $ as the \emph{condition number} of the
problem (\ref{due}) (see \cite{Burgisser2013} for the definition of
condition number of a general problem). It is the worst condition number $%
K(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0)$ as the direction of perturbation $\widehat{%
z}_0$ varies.

The paper \cite{Maset2018} analyzed the condition numbers (\ref{Ktayz0}%
) and (\ref{KAy0}) for $A $ normal and the Euclidean norm as vector norm. The present paper looks at the general case where $A$ and the vector norm are arbitrary.



\subsection{Normwise and componentwise relative errors} \label{nwcw}

We have introduced the \emph{normwise} relative errors (\ref{varepsilon}) and (\ref{deltat}),  but it may also be worthwhile to consider the \emph{componentwise} relative errors
\begin{equation*}
\varepsilon_\ell =\frac{\left\vert \widetilde{y}_{0\ell}-y_{0l}\right\vert }{%
\left\vert y_{0\ell}\right\vert },\ \ell\in\{1,\ldots,n\}, \label{cre0}
\end{equation*}%
of the perturbed initial value and 
\begin{equation*}
\delta_\ell \left( t\right) =\frac{\left\vert \widetilde{y}_\ell\left( t\right)
-y_\ell\left( t\right) \right\vert }{\left\vert y_\ell\left( t\right) \right\vert },\ \ell\in\{1,\ldots,n\}, \label{cre}
\end{equation*}
of the perturbed solution, where $y_{0\ell}$, $\widetilde{y}_{0\ell}$, $y_{\ell}(t)$ and $\widetilde{y}_{\ell}(t)$, $\ell\in\{1,\ldots,n\}$, are the components of $y_{0}$, $\widetilde{y}_{0}$, $y(t)$ and $\widetilde{y}(t)$, respectively. Componentwise relative errors were considered in \cite{FarooqMaset2021-2}, for $A$ diagonalizable. 

We can derive information about the componentwise relative errors from the normwise relative errors. In fact, if the vector norm $\left\Vert \ \cdot \ \right\Vert$ is a $p$-norm, then :
\begin{itemize}
\item [1)]
$
\varepsilon\leq \max\limits_{\ell\in\{1,\ldots,n\}}\varepsilon_l
$
\item [2)]
$
 \delta(t)\leq \max\limits_{\ell\in\{1,\ldots,n\}}\delta_\ell \left( t\right)
$
\item [3)] 
$
\delta_\ell \left( t\right)\leq \frac{\Vert y(t)\Vert}{\vert y_\ell(t)\vert}\delta(t),\ \ell\in\{1,\ldots,n\}.
$
\end{itemize}
Observe that 1), 2) and 3) imply, respectively:
\begin{itemize}
\item [1')] if all the componentwise relative errors $\varepsilon_\ell$  are small, then the normwise relative error $\varepsilon$ is small;
\item [2')] if the normwise relative error $\delta(t)$ is large, then some componentwise relative error $\delta_\ell(t)$ is large;
\item [3')] if the normwise relative error $\delta(t)$ is small, then the componentwise relative error $\varepsilon_\ell$ is small for components $y_\ell(t)$ with a non-large ratio $\frac{\Vert y(t)\Vert}{\vert y_\ell(t)\vert}$.
\end{itemize}
Point 3) (see 3')) is useful for estimating the order of magnitude of the relative error of perturbed components $\widetilde{y}_l(t)$, once the order of magnitude of $\delta(t)$ is known. In light of this, an important question is to understand how frequently components with a large ratio $\frac{\Vert y(t)\Vert}{\vert y_\ell(t)\vert}$ appear. The first of the following two examples shows that, in the GDP-ND model, components with a large ratio rarely occur. Moreover, the second example demonstrates that for a randomly selected matrix $A$, the number of components with a large ratio $\frac{\Vert y(t)\Vert_\infty}{\vert y_\ell(t)\vert}$ is not a substantial percentage of the total number of components. Observe that in 3), we have (recall that $\left\Vert \ \cdot \ \right\Vert$ is a $p$-norm)
\begin{equation}
\frac{\Vert y(t)\Vert}{\vert y_\ell(t)\vert}\leq n^{\frac{1}{p}}\frac{\Vert y(t)\Vert_\infty}{\vert y_\ell(t)\vert}. \label{inftynorm}
\end{equation}
\begin{example}
Consider the GDP-ND model. We look at $10\ 000$ instances of (\ref{ODEdebt}), where the coefficients $a_{11}$, $-a_{12}$, $a_{21}$ and $-a_{11}$ are sampled from the uniform distribution on the interval $[0.01,0.10]$ and the initial value has $Q(0)=1$ and $B(0)$ sampled from the uniform distribution on $[0,1]$. Figure \ref{QBcomp} shows the maximum ratio
\begin{equation}
R=\max\left\{\frac{\Vert (Q(50),B(50))\Vert_2}{\vert Q(50)\vert},\frac{\Vert (Q(50),B(50))\Vert_2}{\vert B(50)\vert}\right\} \label{maximumratioR}
\end{equation}
in logarithmic scale for these $10\ 000$ instances.
