Moreover, the example in Subsection \ref{hnn} also highlights that the ratios $\frac{f_j}{f_1}$ can be significantly smaller than the values of $f_j$.

\subsection{Normal matrices}
Once again, assume that the vector norm is the Euclidean norm and consider $A$ normal.

In the case where $\Lambda_1$ is complex, we have $V_1=W_1=0$. Consequently, the oscillating term $\mathrm{OT}(t,y_0)$ has a constant in time value of $\sqrt{\frac{1}{2}}$ and $K_{\infty}(t,y_0)$ has a constant in time value of $\frac{1}{\vert \widehat{w}^{(1)} \widehat{y}_0 \vert} \sqrt{\frac{1}{2}}$. Note that, in the case where $\Lambda_1$ is real, the constant in time $K_{\infty}(y_0)$ has a value of $\frac{1}{\vert \widehat{w}^{(1)} \widehat{y}_0 \vert} $.

Moreover, since $\frac{f_j}{f_1} = 1$ for $j \in \{2, \ldots, q\}$, and $V_1 = 0$ in the case where $\Lambda_1$ is complex, an earlier onset of the asymptotic behavior for $K(t,y_0)$ is expected compared to non-normal matrices (see (\ref{epsilon<}) and (\ref{epsilonc<})). 

Finally, the constant in time asymptotic condition number is not only the limit as $t\rightarrow +\infty$ of $K(t,y_0)$, but it is also the supremum of $K(t,y_0)$ for $t\geq 0$ (see \cite{Maset2018}).





\section{Examples}\label{Examples}

To illustrate the analysis given in this paper, we consider seven examples of a real ODE (\ref{ode}), where the first two are the models of Section 1 revisited.








\subsection{Gross Domestic Product and National Debt}

The first example is the ODE (\ref{ODEdebt}) of the GDP-ND model of Section 1. Assume, as in the instance (\ref{instanceGDPND}), that there are two real eigenvalues. Moreover, since we set $Q(0)=1$, there is uncertainty only on $B(0)$ and then the direction of perturbation is $\widehat{z}_0=(0,\pm 1)$ (in any vector $p$-norm). In the Euclidean norm, we have
\begin{equation*}
	K_{\infty}(y_{0},\widehat{z}_{0})=\frac{\left\vert \widehat{w}^{(1)}%
		\widehat{z}_{0}\right\vert}{\left\vert \widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{y}%
		_{0}\right\vert}=\sqrt{1+B(0)^2}\cdot \frac{1}{\left\vert\frac{w^{(1)}_1}{w^{(1)}_2}+B(0)\right\vert}. 
\end{equation*}
In the instance (\ref{instanceGDPND}), we have $\frac{w^{(1)}_1}{w^{(1)}_2}=-1$ and so
\begin{equation}
	K_{\infty}(y_{0},\widehat{z}_{0})=\sqrt{1+B(0)^2}\cdot \frac{1}{\vert -1 +B(0)\vert}. \label{expression}
\end{equation}
For the initial ND $B(0)=0.61$ considered in Section 1, we have $K_{\infty}(y_{0},\widehat{z}_{0})=3.0035$.

In Figure \ref{Figura55}, which extends Figure \ref{Figura5} to the interval $[0,150]$ (with a characteristic time $20$ in this context), we can observe how the relative error $\delta(t)$ tends to asymptotically approach the value $0.025641$. We have, remind (\ref{magnification}) and $\varepsilon=\delta(0)$,
$$
\delta(t)=K(t,y_{0},\widehat{z}_{0})\cdot \delta(0)\text{\ \ and\ \ }\lim\limits_{t\rightarrow +\infty}\delta(t)=K_{\infty}(y_{0},\widehat{z}_{0})\cdot \delta(0)=3.0035\cdot \delta(0).
$$

The expression for $K_{\infty}(y_{0},\widehat{z}_{0})$ in (\ref{expression}) has a singularity at $B(0)=1$. In this case, where the initial value $(Q(0),B(0))=(1,1)$ has zero component along the rightmost eigenvector, the asymptotic condition number  $K_{\infty}(t,y_{0},\widehat{z}_{0})$ grows exponentially in time (see \cite{Maset2022}).

For $(Q(0),B(0))=(1,1)$ and $(\widetilde{Q}(0),\widetilde{B}(0))=(1,0.99)$, Figures \ref{QB11}, \ref{Figura311}, and \ref{Figura511} depict the same information as Figures \ref{QB2}, \ref{Figura3}, and \ref{Figura5} do for $(Q(0),B(0))=(1,0.61)$ and $(\widetilde{Q}(0),\widetilde{B}(0))=(1,0.60)$. The differences between the two cases are quite evident. After $50\ \mathrm{yr}$, the relative error  is about $12$ times the initial relative error.


