Note that the high non-normality does not have a critical impact, as is the case with $\Vert \mathrm{e}^{tA}\Vert_2$. In fact, we can observe the following.
\begin{itemize}
	\item [1)] Although $K(t,y_0)$ oscillates similarly to $\Vert \mathrm{e}^{tA}\Vert_2$ as $t$ varies, the maximum of $K(t,y_0)$ has the same order of magnitude as $K_\infty(y_0)$. The high value of $K_\infty(y_0)$ in the top figure is due to a small value of $\vert \widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{y}_0\vert$, rather than high non-normality.
