\subsection{A non-diagonalizable matrix $A$}

We consider a real non-diagonalizable matrix $A$ of order $3$ with two distinct real eigenvalues: the defective eigenvalue $%
r _{1}$ with one Jordan mini-block of dimension $2$ and the non-defective eigenvalue $r_{2}$, where $r_2<r_1$. By recalling point 3 after Theorem \ref{Theorem4}, under the RLGE condition $w^{\left(1,1,2\right) }y_0\neq 0$  we have
\begin{equation*}
	K_{\infty }(y_{0})=\frac{1}{\left\vert \widehat{w}^{\left(1,1,2\right) }\widehat{y}%
		_{0}\right\vert },
\end{equation*}
where
$$
\widehat{w}^{(1,1,2)}=\frac{w^{(1,1,2)}}{\Vert w^{(1,1,2)} \Vert}
$$
and $\Vert w^{(1,1,2)} \Vert$ is the induced norm of the row $w^{(1,1,2)}$.

We consider the case where $r_1=0$, $r_2=-1$,  
\begin{equation*}
	V=\left[ 
	\begin{array}{rrr}
		0 & 0 & 1 \\ 
		1 & 0 & -1 \\ 
		-1 & 1 & 0%
	\end{array}%
	\right] \text{\ \ and\ then\ \ }V^{-1}=\left[ 
	\begin{array}{ccc}
		1 & 1 & 0 \\ 
		1 & 1 & 1 \\ 
		1 & 0 & 0%
	\end{array}%
	\right].
\end{equation*}
The matrix $A$ is
\begin{equation}
	A=\left[ 
	\begin{array}{rrr}
		-1 & 0 & 0 \\ 
		2 & 1 & 1 \\ 
		-1 & -1 & -1%
	\end{array}%
	\right].    \label{matrixJordan}
\end{equation}

The $1$-norm is used as vector norm. We have $w^{(1,1,2)}=[\ 1\ \ 1\ \ 1\ ]$ and then $\widehat{w}^{(1,1,2)}=w^{(1,1,2)}$ since
$\Vert w^{(1,1,2)} \Vert=\left\Vert \left(w^{(1,1,2)}\right)^T\right\Vert_\infty=1$. In Figure \ref{FigureJ}, we see $K(t,y_{0})$ and
$$
K_{\infty }(y_{0})=\frac{\Vert y_0\Vert_1}{\vert y_{01}+y_{02}+y_{03}\vert}=\frac{\vert y_{01}\vert+\vert y_{02}\vert +\vert y_{03}\vert}{\vert y_{01}+y_{02}+y_{03}\vert}
$$ 
for $t\in\left[ 0,50\right] $, for three different initial values $y_{0}$. We can observe an approach of $K(t,y_{0})$ to $K_\infty(y_{0})$, as $t\rightarrow +\infty$, slower than the previous examples. The curves \(K(t, y_0)\) appear, for $t$ close to $50$, nearly parallel to the lines representing their asymptotic value, with a small gap remaining between them. Indeed, we have an $\frac{1}{t}$-convergence of $K(t,y_0)$ to $K_\infty(y_0)$ as $t\rightarrow +\infty $ (see \cite{Maset2022}), instead of the exponential convergence seen in the previous examples.

