From this experimentation, we can conclude that, at least for the vector $p$-norm, where $p\in\{1,2,\infty\}$,  the ratio $R$ is very rarely much larger than $1$. Indeed, it is never much larger than $1$ in these $10\ 000$ instances. Therefore, we have strong evidence that \emph{in the vast majority of cases
\begin{equation}
\max\limits_{t\geq 0}K(t,y_0) \ \text{and} \ \max\limits_{t\geq 0}K_\infty(t,y_0)\label{KandK}
\end{equation}
are of the same order of magnitude. Consequently, in the vast majority of cases, the problem (\ref{due}) is well-conditioned if and only if it is asymptotically well-conditioned}.

Thus, for the vector $p$-norm, $p\in\{1,2,\infty\}$, we have strong evidence that in the vast majority of cases of a rightmost real eigenvalue
\begin{equation}
	\max\limits_{t\geq 0}K(t,y_0) \ \text{and} \ K_\infty(y_0)=\frac{1}{\vert \widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{y}_0\vert}=\frac{\left\Vert w^{(1)} \right\Vert \left\Vert y_0 \right\Vert_2}{\vert  w^{(1)} y_0\vert }  \label{KandK+}
\end{equation}
are of the same order of magnitude. Consequently, in the vast majority of cases of a rightmost real eigenvalue, the problem (\ref{due}) is well-conditioned if and only if $\vert \widehat{w}^{1}\widehat{y}_0\vert$ is not small.



Moreover, for the Euclidean norm, by recalling Subsection \ref{V1close1} we have strong evidence that in the vast majority of cases of a rightmost complex conjugate pair of eigenvalues
\begin{equation*}
\max\limits_{t\geq 0}K(t,y_0) \ \text{and} \ \max\limits_{t\geq 0}K_\infty(t,y_0)\text{\ \ and\ \ }\mathrm{OSF}(y_0)=\frac{1}{\vert \widehat{w}^{(1)}\widehat{y}_0\vert}=\frac{\left\Vert w^{(1)} \right\Vert_2 \left\Vert y_0 \right\Vert_2}{\vert  w^{(1)} y_0\vert } 
\end{equation*}
are of the same order of magnitude. Consequently, in the vast majority of cases of a rightmost complex conjugate pair of eigenvalues, the problem (\ref{due}) is well-conditioned if and only if $\vert \widehat{w}^{1}\widehat{y}_0\vert$ is not small.

\subsection{Highly non-normal matrices} \label{Hnn}

