\section{Non-normal dynamics}

Suppose we want to simulate the transient phase of a real-world system, where this transient $y$ satisfies an ODE (\ref{ode}) with a non-normal stable matrix $A$, where \emph{stable} means that all the eigenvalues of $A$ have negative real part. For a non-linear real-world system, this ODE is the linearization around  an asymptotically stable equilibrium of a non-linear ODE. 

Due to the non-normality of the matrix $A$, it is expected that the transient exhibits an initial (possibly large) growth before decaying to zero. This phenomenon is what characterizes \emph{non-normal dynamics} and can destabilize a non-linear system subject to small perturbation from the asymptotically stable equilibrium (see \cite{Trefethen2005} and \cite{Asilani2018}).



\emph{Suppose that, through simulation, we need to determine the maximum growth of the transient, i.e. $\max_{t\geq 0} \Vert y(t) \Vert$. Additionally, suppose there is uncertainty in the initial value of our simulation}. Here, we consider $y_0$ as the initial value available for the simulation and $\widetilde{y}_0$ as the actual initial value.

\emph{Since there are uncertainties in the initial value, it is important to assess how close the simulation result $\max_{t\geq 0} \Vert y(t) \Vert$ is to the actual maximum growth of the transient, i.e. $\max_{t\geq 0} \Vert \widetilde{y}(t) \Vert$.} 


Information on relative closeness can be obtained as follows. Since
$$
\frac{\Vert \widetilde{y}(t)\Vert }{\Vert y(t)\Vert}=1+\chi(t),
$$
where
$$
\vert \chi(t)\vert \leq \delta(t)=K(t,y_0,\widehat{z}_0)\varepsilon\leq K(t,y_0)\varepsilon
$$
(see (\ref{magnification})), we have
$$
\left\vert \frac{\max\limits_{t\geq 0} \Vert \widetilde{y}(t) \Vert}{\max\limits_{t\geq 0} \Vert y(t) \Vert}-1\right\vert\leq E
$$
whenever $E<1$, where
\begin{equation}
E=\max\limits_{t\geq 0}K(t,y_0)\cdot \varepsilon. \label{E}
\end{equation}
The quantity $E$ is an upper bound for the relative error of the actual maximum transient growth with respect to the simulated maximum transient growth, demonstrating how the condition number $K(t,y_0)$ introduced in the present paper can be useful in the study of non-normal dynamics.

In the case of the vector $p$-norm, $p\in\{1,2,\infty\}$, the available evidence of Section 8, in particular that in Subsection \ref{Hnn}, shows that in the vast majority of cases the quantities in (\ref{KandK}) are of the same order of magnitude. Consequently, we conclude that, in the vast majority of cases, $E$ in (\ref{E}) and
\begin{equation}
E_\infty=\max\limits_{t\geq 0}K_\infty(t,y_0)\cdot \varepsilon \label{Einfty}
\end{equation}
are of the same order of magnitude. Therefore, also the asymptotic condition number $K_\infty(t,y_0)$ introduced and analyzed in this paper can be useful in the study of non-normal dynamics.

\begin{example}
	Consider an ODE (\ref{ode}) with
	\begin{equation}
A=\left[ 
\begin{array}{rr}
	-1 & a \\ 
	0 & -2 
\end{array}%
\right], \label{Annormal}
\end{equation}
where $a>0$ is large. In this example,  the $\infty$-norm is used as vector norm.

The matrix $A$ represents a classical example of an highly non-normal stable matrix (see the ``quiz'' at the beginning of \cite{Trefethen2005}), exibiting a large initial  growth of the solution of (\ref{ode}). In Figure \ref{three-expm}, for $a=50,500,5000$, we see that $\Vert \mathrm{e}^{tA}\Vert_\infty$ initially grows significantly over time before eventually decaying to zero.



Since  
$$  
\left\vert \max_{t\geq 0} \Vert \widetilde{y}(t) \Vert_\infty - \max_{t\geq 0} \Vert y(t) \Vert_\infty \right\vert \leq \max_{t\geq 0} \Vert \mathrm{e}^{tA} \Vert_\infty \Vert \widetilde{y}_0 - y_0 \Vert_\infty,  
$$  
it is expected that $\max_{t \geq 0} \Vert \widetilde{y}(t) \Vert_\infty$ will not be as close to $\max_{t \geq 0} \Vert y(t) \Vert_\infty$ as $\widetilde{y}_0$ is to $y_0$, when the closeness is measured in terms of absolute error.


On the other hand, as we will see below, this is not the case when the closeness is measured in terms of relative error instead of absolute error. Therefore, if controlling the relative error of \(\max_{t \geq 0} \Vert \widetilde{y}(t) \Vert_\infty\) is important, considering the growth of the matrix exponential \(\mathrm{e}^{tA}\) can be misleading, as it pertains to the absolute error rather than the relative error.

