\begin{abstract}
  % The development of robotic applications and the widespread of different computing systems have led to the formation of multi-robot systems (MRS) to complete various tasks in the real world. Many MRS are implemented on top of software middleware like ROS (robot operating system). However, ROS's natural communication pattern,  publish/subscribe lacks the flexibility to retrieve arbitrary data from arbitrary robots in an MRS. Moreover, the storage format of ROS also has a poor performance in data query operations.
We present ROSfs, a novel user-level file system for the Robot Operating System (ROS). ROSfs interprets a robot file as a group of sub-files, with each having a distinct label. ROSfs applies a time index structure to enhance the flexible data query while the data file is under modification. It provides multi-robot systems (MRS) with prompt cross-robot data acquisition and collaboration. We implemented a ROSfs prototype and integrated it into a mainstream ROS platform. We then applied and evaluated ROSfs on real-world UAVs and data servers. Evaluation results show that compared with traditional ROS storage methods, ROSfs improves the offline query performance by up to 129x and reduces inter-robot online data query latency under a wireless network by up to 7x.
%Multi-robot systems (MRS) are expected to have various data collaborations among robots, which requires efficient data management. We observe that the existing data storage formats and message-passing models in ROS (robot operating system) cannot meet the needs of MRS. Hence, we present ROSfs, a user-level file system for ROS. It provides robots with efficient onboard data management and optimizes offline data query performance. Moreover, ROSfs proposes an optimized indexing mechanism to enable the on-disk data to be transmitted under the wireless network. We evaluate ROSfs on storage servers and real-world unmanned aerial vehicles (drones). Evaluation results show that ROSfs improves the offline data query performance by up to 129x, and enhances online data query under the wireless network by up to 3.2x compared to ROS.
\end{abstract}

