\subsubsection{Maximum degree} \label{subsubsec: max degree}
The theorem below, due to~\cite{hex}, shows that GG is PSPACE-complete even when played on graphs with maximum degree 3, maximum indegree 2 and maximum outdegree 2. The reduction works by straightforwardly replacing each node that violates these conditions with a set of nodes that satisfies them, without changing the outcome of the game. 

\begin{theorem}[S. Reisch, 1981] \label{thm: gg final version}
GG is PSPACE-complete even when played on graphs with maximum degree 3, maximum indegree 2 and maximum outdegree 2.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
We replace all nodes with indegree~3 by an equivalent chain of nodes with indegree~2 or less (see Figure~\ref{fig: indegree}). Indegree above~3 can be handled similarly with a longer chain. The treatment of nodes with outdegree~3 or more can be seen by reversing all arrows in the diagram. The final case of indegree and outdegree exactly~2 (making total degree~4) is similarly straightforward. \end{proof}


