The special dead piece labeled \emph{wB 3bB bQ} (see Section~\ref{subsubsec: dead}, item~\ref{special dead piece}) consists of a white Beetle on top of 3 black Beetles and a black Queen on the bottom. This tower of pieces has two functions. In the first place, naturally, it is a dead piece. We see this because the top piece (white Beetle) can make no valuable move in one turn, and if it ever would move, the black Beetle that was beneath it would be able to jump on top, leaving the next black Beetle free to, without restraint, find a white Queen to surround in the next moves. In the second place, the tower ensures that black Grasshopper 1 cannot jump over white Spider 1, lest the white Beetle step left and surround the black Queen. Therefore, it is forced to proceed forward to the next structure.



Having described the flow of play in the \emph{quantifier} stage, we skip the \emph{clause chooser} and \emph{literal chooser} stages for the time being and proceed directly to the \emph{tester} stage. Later we discuss the intermediate stages.

\begin{lemma}\label{lem: first quantifier}
If a literal $l \in 
\{v_1,\neg v_1\}$ is chosen during the \emph{literal chooser} stage, the $\exists$-player (Black) has a winning strategy if and only if $l$ is true under the variable assignment chosen during the \emph{quantifier} stage.
\end{lemma}




\begin{proof}
The proof of this lemma relies on the following claim, which we verify only later when discussing the \emph{literal chooser} gadget and how the various gadgets throughout the position connect together (Section~\ref{subsubsec: remarks one Hive rule}).

\begin{claim}\label{claim: tester grasshoppers}
In the \emph{tester} stage, a Grasshopper enters into the \emph{quantifier/tester} gadget for a variable $v_i$ through that gadget's lower- (respectively, upper-) left entrance if and only if the literal $v_i$ (respectively, $\neg v_i$) was chosen during the \emph{literal chooser} stage.
\end{claim}


Suppose, then, that the chosen literal $l$ is indeed either $v_1$ or $\neg v_1$, and that white Grasshopper~1 or~2, respectively, has therefore entered the \emph{tester.} The following case analysis 
now shows that 
the $\exists$-player (Black) has a winning strategy if and only if the chosen literal from the \emph{literal chooser}  
is true under the value assigned earlier to $v_1$ in the \emph{quantifier} stage. There are four cases.
\begin{itemize}
    \item[Case 1 ($v_1$ {\tt true,} $v_1$ chosen):] Suppose the variable was assigned {\tt true} at the \emph{quantifier} stage and the $\exists$-player chooses the literal $v_1$ at the \emph{literal chooser} stage. Figure \ref{fig: tester Qtrue Vtrue} shows a close-up of the lower (``true'') region from Figure~\ref{fig: E-quantifier}, before the white Grasshopper 1 enters, with the rest of the pieces locked down. When white Grasshopper 1 enters from the left and lands on the indicated space, it frees black Ant 1 to surround the white Queen by moving two hexes down. Thus the $\exists$-player has a winning strategy. 
