\subsubsection{Gap} \label{subsubsec: gap}
To ensure that all pieces locked by the One Hive rule are really locked, it is necessary at some places to disconnect two structures by including 
a \emph{gap.} There are three types of places where we need a \emph{gap.} First, a \emph{gap} is required in one of the two paths between two \emph{quantifier} gadgets, to ensure that the Hive is connected in only one place. Second, we need a \emph{gap} wherever a \emph{literal chooser} (or a \emph{join} that has connected two \emph{literal choosers}) would meet a \emph{tester} gadget. Third, a \emph{gap} is employed whenever a crossing occurs. The first two uses of \emph{gaps} are treated in Section~\ref{subsubsec: remarks one Hive rule}. The third is treated in Section~\ref{subsubsec: crossings}.

In Figure~\ref{fig: gap}, White Grasshopper 1 comes in from the previous structure to land on space \textcolor{nicegreen}{1}. This frees the black Spider, which can pass over the gap by following the arrow and move to space \textcolor{nicegreen}{2} (moving left would be selfmate). This move frees white Grasshopper 2, which jumps away to the next structure (jumping elsewhere loses; the piece marked ``wb 6bB wQ'' is just a standard dead edge piece, explicitly labeled to emphasize the presence of a white Queen under the Beetles, which Grasshopper~2 may not surround).

