\begin{abstract}
    % 50-150 words
\noindent
We propose a citation index $\nu$ (``nu'') and show that it lies between the classical $h$-index and $g$-index. This idea is then generalized to a monotone parametric family $(\nu_\alpha)$ ($\alpha\ge 0$), whereby $h=\nu_0$ and  $\nu=\nu_1$, while the limiting value $\nu_\infty$ is expressed in terms of the maximum citation.
\end{abstract}

\subsection*{1. Introduction} \label{intro}
% backgroud  adn purpose of the study

\cite{hirsch2005index} made a breakthrough in scientometrics by proposing for the first time a simple citation index (commonly referred to as \emph{$h$-index}), which had an advantage of aggregating the author's productivity on the basis of both the number of published papers and their quality. Before that, only some extensive summary statistics were used, such as the mean number of citations per paper. Since then, the $h$-index has become a standard metric of authors' reputation and productivity, for instance routinely taken into consideration in academic appointments and promotions.

Specifically, the $h$-index is defined as the maximum number $h$ of an author's papers, each cited at least $h$ times \citep{hirsch2005index}.
Therefore, this index only takes into account the fact of a relatively ``high'' citation of a paper, but the actual number of citations of such a paper is effectively ignored.

To remedy such censoring of larger citations,
the alternative citation index (referred to as \emph{$g$-index}) was proposed by \citet{egghe2006improvement}, defined as the maximum number $g$ of an author's most cited papers, such that their total number of citations is at least $g^2$. From this definition, it is easy to see that $h\le g$ \citep{egghe2006theory}.

These two (by now classical) indexes have attracted a lot of interest and generated ample research into their analytic properties and performance on real datasets, including their estimation in a variety of statistical models of count data (see, e.g., \cite{er2006, egghe2006theory, egghe2008h, yong2014critique}).
Furthermore, many modifications and alternative variants of the $h$ and $g$ indexes have been proposed, focusing on certain features of the citation profile (see, e.g.,  \cite{alonso2009h, guns2009real, hirsch2019index} and further references therein). An up-to-date online resource \cite{web-h} is especially useful by keeping track of the state-of-the-art in this domain.

In the present work, we introduce a new citation index $\nu$ (``nu'') aiming to bridge the mathematical definitions of Hirsh's $h$ and Egghi's $g$. This idea was first coined in \cite{Nuer2023}. Namely, we start by observing that the $h$-index can be represented as a sum of certain indicator functions that censor papers to ensure a required minimum of citations. Building on this observation, our $\nu$-index essentially mimics the summative nature of the $h$-index but the new summation explicitly involves the citation numbers of the top papers.

We are then able to show that our $\nu$ is ``sandwiched'' between $h$ and $g$; more precisely, we prove the two-sided inequalities
\begin{equation}\label{eq:h<nu<g*}
h\le \nu\le g^*,
\end{equation}
where $g^*$ denotes a modified (unconstrained) $g$-index, obtained if we are allowed to add fictitious zeros to the citation vector  \citep{woeginger2008axiomatic}.

On the other hand, a ``tempered'' version $\bar{\nu}$ of the $\nu$-index, modified so as to be not larger than the number of published papers, satisfies the inequalities
\begin{equation}\label{eq:h<bar-nu<g}
h\le \bar{\nu}\le g.
\end{equation}
We will finish off by introducing a more general family of citation indexes $(\nu_\alpha)$, where $\nu_\alpha$ is a non-decreasing (integer-valued) function of 
a real parameter $\alpha\ge 0$. Here, $h=\nu_0$ and $\nu=\nu_1$, while  the limiting value $\nu_\infty$ can be expressed in terms of the maximum citation. Concluding remarks will touch upon %estimation 
evaluation of citation indexes in specific frequency models using the concept of \emph{limit shape} \citep{nuerISSI2021,bogachevGIGP,Nuer2023,yong2014critique}.


