\subsection*{2. The $h$ and $g$ indexes}\label{sec:3.4.1}

Let us fix some  notation. Suppose that an author has published $m\ge1$ papers, with the ordered numbers of citations $x_1\ge x_2\ge \dots \ge x_m$, where $x_i\ge0$ are integers (possibly zero). We call  $\boldsymbol{x}=(x_1,x_2,\dots,x_m)$ the \emph{citation vector}. Note that $n=\sum_{i=1}^mx_i$ is the total number of citations. A zero vector $\boldsymbol{0}=(0,\dots,0)$ represents the degenerate case of no citations (note that the variable dimension of this vector is determined by the number of published papers).

We say that a vector $\boldsymbol{x}=(x_1,\dots,x_m)$ is dominated by a vector $\boldsymbol{y}=(y_1,\dots,y_{\ell})$ (written as $\boldsymbol{x}\preccurlyeq\boldsymbol{y}$) if $x_i\le y_i$ for all $i\ge1$; more precisely, if $m\le \ell$ then $x_i\le y_i$ for $i=1,\dots,m$, but if $m>\ell$ then $x_i\le y_i$ for $i=1,\dots,\ell$ \emph{and} $x_i=0$ for $i=\ell+1,\dots,m$. Effectively, these two cases imply that we complement the absent components of either $\boldsymbol{x}$ or $\boldsymbol{y}$ with fictitious zeros to equalize their dimensions, and then the dominance holds component-wise.

The following natural conditions are commonly assumed to be satisfied by any reasonable citation index $c(\boldsymbol{x})$ (see \cite{woeginger2008axiomatic}; cf.\ \citet{woeginger2008axH}):
\begin{itemize}
\item[(C1)] If $\boldsymbol{x}=\boldsymbol{0}$ then $c(\boldsymbol{x})=0$.

\item[(C2)] If $\boldsymbol{x}=(x_1, x_2, \dots, x_m)$ and $\boldsymbol{y}=(x_1, x_2, \dots, x_m,0)$ then $c(\boldsymbol{x})=c(\boldsymbol{y})$.

\item[(C3)] If $\boldsymbol{x}\preccurlyeq \boldsymbol{y}$ then $c(\boldsymbol{x})\le c(\boldsymbol{y})$.
\end{itemize}

Let us now recall the above verbal definitions of the $h$ and $g$ indexes and put them into an explicit mathematical formulation.

Starting with the $h$-index, its definition can be expressed as follows,
\begin{equation}
    h\equiv h(\boldsymbol{x})=\max \left\{ j\ge 1 \colon\! \sum_{i=1}^{m} {\bf 1}_{\{x_i\ge j\}}\ge j \right\}\!,
\label{eq:hindexDF}
\end{equation}
where the indicator notation ${\bf 1}_{A_j}$ is defined as ${\bf 1}_{A_j}=1$ if the condition $A_j$ is satisfied, and ${\bf 1}_{A_j}=0$ otherwise.

Note that the maximum in \eqref{eq:hindexDF} is uniquely defined, since the sum on the left-hand side is a decreasing function of $j$, while the right-hand is strictly increasing.

Thus, the sum in \eqref{eq:hindexDF}, for a given $j$, represents the number of papers with at least $j$ citations, while the inequality requires that this number is not smaller than $j$. In particular, noting that the sum of indicators in \eqref{eq:hindexDF} is not larger than $m$, it follows that the $h$-index is bounded by the number of papers:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:h<m}
h\le m.
\end{equation}
In the degenerate case with $x_i\equiv 0$ (i.e., $\boldsymbol{x}=\boldsymbol{0}$), the inequality in \eqref{eq:hindexDF} is only satisfied for the value $j=0$, which is excluded from the testing range; thus, the resulting set of suitable $j$'s is empty and, according to the common convention, its maximum is set to be zero: $\max\varnothing = 0$; hence
\begin{equation}\label{eq:h0}
h(\boldsymbol{0})=0,
\end{equation}
so that property (C1) is automatically satisfied. It is also easy to see that (C2) holds (because $h$ is insensitive to zero citations) and that (C3) is also true.


