\subsection*{4. Parametric family $(\nu_\alpha)$}

It is quite natural to generalize the definition of the index $\nu$ in  \eqref{eq:gindexDF-old} by considering different powers. Namely, for $\alpha\ge0$ we define the $\nu_\alpha$-index as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:nu_alpha}
\nu_\alpha\equiv \nu_\alpha(\boldsymbol{x})=\max\left\{j\ge 1\colon \sum_{i=1}^{m} x_i^\alpha\mypp {\bf 1}_{\{x_i\ge j\}}\ge j^{\alpha+1}\right\}\!.
\end{equation}
Clearly, for $\alpha=0$ and $\alpha=1$ this definition is reduced to \eqref{eq:hindexDF} and \eqref{eq:gindexDF-old}, respectively:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:h=nu0}
h=\nu_0, \qquad \nu=\nu_1.
\end{equation}
Like in \eqref{eq:hindexDF} and \eqref{eq:gindexDF-old}, the existence and uniqueness of the maximum $\nu_\alpha$ is self-evident, noting that the sum is a decreasing function of $j$ while the right-hand side is strictly increasing. It is also straightforward to verify that $\nu_\alpha$ satisfies (C1)--(C3). We also observe the monotonicity of the family $(\nu_\alpha)$.
\begin{lemma}
The function $\nu_\alpha$ is increasing in $\alpha\ge0$.
\end{lemma}
\proof
Rewrite \eqref{eq:nu_alpha} as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:nu-alpha-norm1}
\nu_\alpha=\max\left\{j\ge 1\colon \sum_{i=1}^{m} \left(\frac{x_i}{j}\right)^{\mynn \alpha} {\bf 1}_{\{x_i\ge j\}}\ge j\right\}\!,
\end{equation}
and note that the sum in \eqref{eq:nu-alpha-norm1} is monotone increasing in $\alpha$, since $x_i/j\ge 1$.
\endproof

It is also interesting to understand the meaning of the limiting value $\nu_\infty$.

\begin{lemma}
For a citation vector $\boldsymbol{x}=(x_1,\dots,x_m)$, denote by $\ell_1=\sum_{i=1}^m {\bf 1}_{\{x_i=x_1\}}$ the multiplicity of the top citation number $x_1$. Then
\begin{equation}
\nu_\infty(\boldsymbol{x})=
\begin{cases}
x_1-1&\text{if }\,\ell_1<x_1,\\
x_1&{\text{otherwise}.}
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
\end{lemma}
\proof
Easily follows using \eqref{eq:nu-alpha-norm1} by noting that $(x_1/j)^\infty$ equals $\infty$, $1$ or $0$ according as $j<x_1$, $j=x_1$ or $j>x_1$, respectively.
\endproof

\subsection*{5. Discussion and conclusions}

We have introduced some new citation indexes starting with $\nu=\nu_1$, and investigated their relations with the classical indexes $h$ and $g$.

As already mentioned, the $h$-index is straightforward and informative, but it is limited by only acknowledging the fact of a high citation but not the actual number of citations. In contrast, the $g$-index is based exclusively on the citations of a few top papers, but ignoring the ``footing'' of lower-cited papers.

Our synthetic proposal of the $\nu$-index is designed so as to take into account both higher and lower cited papers, which may assess the individual's productivity in a more fair and balanced way. Indeed, we have seen that the $\nu$-index is in a sense bridging Hirsch's $h$ and Egghe's $g$.

Furthermore, the spectrum of the indexes $(\nu_\alpha)$ provides a flexible toolkit that allows one either to enhance or to inhibit the input from top-cited papers, as required.

Of course, it goes without saying that none of these, or any other indexes known in the literature is perfect and should replace the rest.
In fact, a reasonable practical recommendation may be to choose a few indexes to judge someone's academic achievement, depending on the assessment requirements and also on the specific features of the scientific domain.

In this regard, it may be useful to choose the parameter $\alpha$ in the index $\nu_\alpha$ according to certain individual features of the citation vector $\boldsymbol{x}$, in the spirit of limit theorems for norms of random vectors \citep{Schlather, Bogachev2006}. We will address this issue in our future work.

As another direction of research, it is important to develop methods of evaluation of a preferred citation index under a suitable statistical model of citation frequencies. We advocate \citep{nuerISSI2021,bogachevGIGP,Nuer2023} the use of the so-called \emph{limit shape} of the random citation profile, which enables one to use an appropriate rescaling of the ``tail'' of the frequency distribution to extract visual information about its behavior. The idea of the limit shape stems from theory of random integer partitions \citep{vershik1996statistical} and was first applied to the citation context by \cite{yong2014critique}.

To give just one example of utility of the limit shape, consider a power law frequency model $f_j= j^{-a}\mynn/\zeta(a)$ ($j\ge 1$), where $\zeta(a)=\sum_{j\ge 1} j^{-a}$ is the Riemann zeta function ($a>1$). \cite{er2006} proposed the following formula for the $h$-index,
\begin{equation}\label{eq:h-est}
h\approx m^{1/a},
\end{equation}
where $m$ is the number of published papers. This expression easily follows upon the replacement of sums emerging in the power law model by the corresponding integrals, which can be explicitly computed.
As an alternative, we argue that, even though the power law is \emph{scale free} (in that $A^{a}f_{\myn Ax}= f_x$,
for any scaling factor $A>0$), the limit shape exists and is given by
the formula
$y=x^{1-a}/c_a$, where $c_a=(a-1)\mypp\zeta(a)>1$.
As a result, the $h$-index can be estimated as follows \citep{Nuer2023},
\begin{equation}\label{eq:h-est*}
h\approx\left(\frac{m}{c_a}\right)^{\mynn 1/a}\!.
\end{equation}
Estimator \eqref{eq:h-est*} is more conservative compared to \eqref{eq:h-est}, but most importantly, it retains the number-theoretic flavor of the original discrete distribution (through the term $\zeta(a)$).

