\section{Conclusion and Future Work}
We propose a neural network verification approach that features adaptive exploration of the sub-problems produced by \bab. \tool is guided by \emph{counterexample potentiality}, so we can efficiently find the sub-problems that contain counterexamples. Experimental evaluation demonstrates the superiority of our proposed approach in efficiency over existing baselines.

%introduces a greedy exploration for the \bab tree generated by \bab algorithm. By evaluating the severity of the sub-problems, which is defined based on the likelihood of containing counterexamples, \tool prioritizes the exploration of more promising branches in \bab tree. Our experimental results demonstrate the effectiveness of \tool across 5 models and 816 problem instances, by a comparison to two state-of-the-art baseline approaches.

As future work, we aim to investigate how our approach can be used to improve ReLU selection heuristics, such as DeepSplit~\cite{henriksen2021deepsplit}, such that we can further accelerate verification.

