      \field{abstract}{Existing techniques for improving datacenter utilization while guaranteeing the {QoS} are based on the assumption that queries have similar behaviors. However, user queries in emerging compute demanding services demonstrate signiﬁcantly diverse behavior and require adaptive parallelism. Our study shows that the end-to-end latency of the compute demanding query is determined together by the system-wide load, its workload, its parallelism, contention on shared cache, and memory bandwidth. When hosting such new services, the current cross-query resource allocation results in either severe {QoS} violation or signiﬁcant resource under-utilization.}
