      \field{abstract}{The introduction of microservices and functions using serverless deployment styles for cloud-native applications will trigger a shift in the orchestration paradigm towards an intent-driven model. In this model we shift from imperatively declaring an object’s state to the declaration of a set of desired intents. Intent-driven orchestration ({IDO}) enables the management of applications through their service level objectives ({SLOs}) while minimizing service owner and administrator overhead. By enabling service owners to express the desired target key performance indicator ({KPI}) objectives for their service components instead of declaratively defining the required state and resources, we enable ease of use and abstraction from underlying platforms. By adding a planning component to a Kubernetes-based orchestration stack, the feasibility of translating service objectives into actionable decisions is demonstrated. As this new architecture component introduces more autonomy in the control plane, a means to evaluate the results of planning is defined.}
