\section{Introduction}

%Mass collection of personal records of individuals allows machine learning algorithms, which sift through personal data to reach conclusions, to play a significant role in carrying out day-to-day tasks in our society. 
%Thus, designing algorithms which strike a favorable balance between drawing accurate conclusions from data and safeguarding the personal information of an individual from misuse, is a key challenge. 


The densest-$k$-subgraph (D$k$S) \cite{feige2001dense} corresponds to a vertex subset $\calS\subset \calV$ of size $k$ (a pre-specified size parameter) with the largest (induced) edge density in a network representation $\calG$ of a dataset.
\DkS{} belongs to the broader family of density based subgraph detection methods,
which constitute a key primitive in graph mining and find practical use in a myriad of application domains ranging from bioinformatics, social network analysis, and fraud detection (see surveys \cite{lee2010survey, gionis2015dense, lanciano2024survey} and references therein.)
%bioinformatics~\cite{jin20093, fratkin2006motifcut, everett2006dense}, social network analysis, e.g.,~\cite{gibson2005discovering, dourisboure2007extraction, gajewar2012multi, angel2012dense, bonchi2016identifying, bonchi2019importance}, and fraud detection, e.g.,~\cite{boginski2003structural, li2020flowscope}; see~\cite{gionis2015dense, lee2010survey, lanciano2024survey} and references therein. 
The \DkS{} formulation is distinct among density based formulations because it allows explicit control of the size of the extracted subgraph. This is in contrast to other approaches which aim to maximize the minimum induced degree (i.e., the $k$-core decomposition \cite{seidman1983network}) or the the average induced degree (i.e., the densest subgraph problem \cite{goldberg1984finding,charikar2000greedy,harb2022faster,boob2020flowless,chekuri2022densest,nguyen2024multiplicative}).
Hence, the utility offered by these algorithms in terms of subgraph density can be low, if the size of the extracted subgraph is large - for practical examples of such occurrences, see  \cite{tsourakakis2013denser,shin2016corescope}.
While this provides motivation for employing \DkS{}, the flip-side is that it is computationally very hard, and good approximation is not possible under certain complexity theoretic assumptions~\cite{manurangsi2017almost}.
That being said, practical approaches \cite{lu2025densest,konar2021exploring,papailiopoulos2014finding} can be used to obtain high-quality approximate solutions to \DkS{} on real-world graphs.

In many applications such as public health, social networks and finance, network datasets comprise sensitive information, and re-identification and other kinds of privacy risks are serious issues \cite{yuan2024unveiling, peng2012two}. 
For instance, consider a social network where the identities of the vertices (i.e., individuals) are public, but the edges (i.e., personal contacts) are private. Note that even the absence of an edge between a pair of individuals can be viewed as private information (e.g., lack of friendship between two prominent persons). Simply keeping the link structure of the social network private (i.e., hiding the contacts from public view) is not sufficient to guarantee privacy, since by observing the output of queries made on the dataset, it is possible for an adversary to verify the presence/absence of edges \cite{jiang2021applications}.
%For instance, an individual might wish to keep their contacts private. 

A number of privacy models have been considered, but Differential Privacy (DP)~\cite{dwork2014algorithmic,dwork2006calibrating} has emerged as a popular and powerful framework for designing algorithms with rigorous privacy guarantees, since it doesn't make any assumptions about the adversary. 
%Here, we study how to solve the \DkS{} problem with DP protection of the edges in $\calG$.
DP algorithms have been developed for many graph problems, ranging from clustering and community detection, \cite{epasto2022scalable,mohamed2022differentially,nguyen2024differentially}, to personalized PageRank \cite{epasto2022differentially,wei2024differentially}, and computing different kinds of graph statistics, e.g.,~\cite{nissim2007smooth, karwa2014private, hay2009accurate, gupta2012iterative, kasiviswanathan2013analyzing, blocki2013differentially} (some of these are for other graph privacy models). Closer to our present work, DP algorithms for the densest subgraph problem \cite{farhadi2022differentially,nguyen2021differentially,dinitz2025almost} and the $k$-core decomposition \cite{dhulipala2022differential} have also been developed.
{\em However, to the best of our knowledge, the D$k$S problem has not been studied under DP.}


% In recent years, DP has been deployed in many areas of machine learning (see \cite{ponomareva2023dp} and references therein), deep learning \cite{abadi2016deep}, and graph mining (see surveys \cite{li2023private,jiang2021applications} and references therein). 


