\noindent {\bf Contributions:} 
We present the first private algorithms for the \DkS{} problem under edge differential privacy (DP) by private computation of principal components of the graph adjacency matrix, building on the non-private approximation algorithm of \cite{papailiopoulos2014finding}. In general, DP algorithms achieve their privacy guarantees by adding controlled amounts of noise to non-private computations. In this regard, existing approaches for private principal components (PPC) can be categorized into one-shot \cite{chaudhuri2012near,kapralov2013differentially,gonem2018smooth} and iterative noise addition schemes \cite{hardt2013beyond,hardt2014noisy,balcan2016improved}. The first category adds noise only once to the non-private PCs, thereby offering scalability. However, the amount of noise required to achieve a target privacy guarantee can be large, thus compromising utility. As we show, existing one-shot approaches for PPC can offer poor utility. Thus, developing a one-shot technique for PPC that balances scalability and utility remains an open problem. This is the central technical challenge that we address in our approach to solving the DP-\DkS{} problem. We additionally consider the iterative private power method \cite{hardt2013beyond,hardt2014noisy,balcan2016improved} for PPC, which adds noise during an optimization process, and can offer better privacy-utility trade-offs but at the expense of higher time complexity. Through experiments on real graphs, we provide compare and contrast the efficacy of these two approaches for DP-\DkS{}
Our main contributions are summarized below.

