\noindent {\bf (1):} 
Our primary approach is based on the technique of {\em output perturbation}, where the principal component (PC) of the adjacency matrix is first computed non-privately followed by a one-shot noise addition step to provide DP. 
We first derive a new $\ell_2$ local sensitivity bound under edge DP via tools from matrix perturbation theory (see \autoref{theo:LS}),
and use this to illustrate the large gap between the global and local sensitivities of the PC on real graph datasets.
This implies that the standard approach of calibrating noise to global sensitivity \cite{dwork2014algorithmic} leads to poor accuracy, motivating the application of instance-based mechanisms such as smooth sensitivity \cite{nissim2007smooth} or Propose-Test-Release \cite{dwork2009differential}. These mechanisms calibrate noise to local sensitivity-based estimates to provide DP, thereby offering improved privacy-utility trade-offs. However, a key challenge is that they are difficult to implement in practice, as they need not involve polynomial-time computations in general.

\noindent {\bf (2):} 
We then derive a tight analysis of prior bounds on computing PPC via smooth sensitivity~\cite{gonem2018smooth} (developed to address the high computational complexity of~\cite{nissim2007smooth}), and show that these are very close to the global sensitivity value, in general (see \autoref{smooth_sens}).

\noindent {\bf (3):} 
Given the unsuitability of smooth sensitivity, we shift our focus to using the Propose-Test-Release (PTR) framework \cite{dwork2009differential, li2024computing} for DP-\DkS{}. At a high level, PTR requires proposing a bound $\beta$ for the local sensitivity of the PC on the given dataset, followed by a differentially private test to see if adding noise to the PC calibrated to $\beta$ would violate privacy.
However, PTR is computationally very expensive in general, and the only prior work for implementing PTR for graph problems in polynomial time was~\cite{li2023private} for an unrelated problem of computing epidemic metrics. 
Our main contributions are a novel technique for computing a lower bound on the distance to instances with local sensitivity bound of $\beta$ (see \autoref{theo:upp_bound}), and a more efficient algorithm for computing the optimal $\beta$ (see \autoref{ptr_success} and Proposition \ref{ptr_parameters}).
\emph{Our methods reduce the complexity of PTR for DP-\DkS{} to basically the same as computation of the PC, which gives us significant improvement in terms of running time}. %this allows us to use current best methods for computing the PC, which can be scaled to very large networks.

\noindent {\bf (4):}  
In our second approach, we adapt the private power method (PPM) of~\cite{hardt2013beyond,hardt2014noisy,balcan2016improved}, which comprises of interleaved steps of matrix vector multiplication followed by noise injection and re-normalization. We show that it has good performance, in general for the \DkS{} problem, for many graphs, though the time complexity is quite high (up to two orders slower than the PTR-based approach).

We test both algorithms for approximate D$k$S on real world graph datasets and demonstrate that it can provide good privacy-utility trade-off. A representative example is provided in Figure \ref{fig:orkut}, which depicts the edge-density versus size trade-off on a social network with $3$ million vertices and $120$ million edges. The yellow line is the rank-$1$ non-private algorithm of \cite{papailiopoulos2014finding}, whereas the red and blue lines depict PTR and PPM respectively. 
% While both algorithms exhibit performance comparable with the non-private implementation, PPM enjoys a benefit over PTR in that it releases one lesser parameter privately (which is reflected in the smaller overall privacy budget). 
While both algorithms exhibit performance comparable with the non-private implementation, PPM incurs a slightly lower privacy cost, and gives better performance in some networks.
However, this comes at the expense of being more computationally intensive than PTR owing to its iterative nature. Our results indicate that the two algorithms effect a trade-off in terms of the overall allocated privacy budget and the computational time requirement. Specifically, if the allocated privacy budget is small, PPM can be applied at the expense of more computational time. On the other hand, if computational time is the bottleneck, PTR can be applied with a slightly larger privacy budget.  

