\section{Non-Private approximate D$k$S via principal components}
\label{non_private_dks}
The main issue with problem \eqref{eq:dks} is that it is NP-hard \cite{feige2001dense}. Moreover, this problem has a well-documented history of being resistant to efficient approximation \cite{khot2006ruling, manurangsi2017almost, bhaskara2012polynomial}. Consequently, we resort to the low-rank approximation scheme of \cite{papailiopoulos2014finding}, which has exhibits good empirical performance and has approximation guarantees. In particular, we review the scheme with rank-$1$ approximation, as this provides a simple and highly scalable solution.

The best rank-one approximation of the adjacency matrix \( \mathbf{A} \) can be obtained using its principal component and is represented as \(\hat{\mathbf{A}} = \lambda_1\mathbf{v}\mathbf{v}^T\), where $\lambda_1 >0 $ is the principal eigen-value and $\vv \in \mathbb{R}^n$ is the principal component. 
Furthermore, since $\vA$ is non-negative, and if the graph $\setG$ represented by $\vA$ is connected, the Perron-Frobenius Theorem \cite[Chapter 7]{meyer2023matrix} implies that $\vv$ is element-wise positive. Letting $\lambda_1 \geq |\lambda_2| \geq \cdots \geq |\lambda_n|$ denote the eigen-values of $\vA$ sorted in descending order, the principal eigen-gap of $\setG$ is defined as ${\sf GAP}(\setG) := \lambda_1 - |\lambda_2|$. 
Applying the rank-one approximation of \(\mathbf{A}\) to the objective function of problem \eqref{eq:dks} is tantamount to solving the problem 
\iffalse
yields the approximation
\begin{equation}
    \max_{\vx \in \setX_k} \lambda_1 (\vv^T\vx)^2
\end{equation}
which simplifies to the problem
\fi
\begin{equation}\label{eq:non-private_baseline}
\max_{\vx \in \setX_k} \vv^T\vx,
\end{equation}
since, as explained previously, $\vv$ is element-wise positive. The problem admits a very simple solution - compute the support of the $k$-largest entries of $\vv$, which can be accomplished in $O(n\log k)$ time. Let $\hat{\vx}_k$ denote the solution of \eqref{eq:non-private_baseline}, which serves as our non-private baseline. In terms of the quality of the approximate solution, the following result is known.
\begin{proposition}[Adapted from~\cite{papailiopoulos2014finding}]
\label{upper_bound}
For any unweighted graph \(\mathcal{G}\), the optimal size-$k$ edge density is no more than
\begin{equation}
d_k^* \leq \min \left\{ \frac{1}{k(k-1)} \hat{\mathbf{x}}_k^{\top} \hat{\mathbf{A}} \hat{\mathbf{x}}_k + \frac{1}{k-1} |\lambda_2|, \frac{1}{k-1} |\lambda_1|, 1 \right\}.
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}
In the following section, we explain our approach to privatizing the algorithm.

\section{Main ideas}

Since the non-private, rank-$1$ approximation approach outlined in the above section is centered around computing the principal component $\vv$ of the graph adjacency matrix $\vA$, a natural means of making the overall procedure DP is to first release a DP version of $\vv$. 
%\anil{this should be $\vv_1$?}
This noisy principal component can be subsequently used to extract an estimate of the size-$k$ densest subgraph in the following manner. Let $\vv_{\textsf{priv}}$ denote a \((\epsilon, \delta)\)-DP estimate of $\vv$. Similar to \eqref{eq:non-private_baseline}, we now solve the following rank-$1$ approximation problem
\begin{equation}\label{eq:noisy_dks}
    \hat{\vx}_k = \arg
    \underset{\vx \in \setX_k}{\max} | \vv_{\textsf{priv}}^T \vx |
\end{equation}
to obtain the final output $\hat{\vx}_k$. Note that the post-processing property of DP implies that $\hat{\vx}_k$ also obeys \((\epsilon, \delta)\)-DP.
Since $\vv_{\textsf{priv}}$ may not be element-wise non-negative in general, the solution of  \eqref{eq:noisy_dks} is different from its non-private counterpart. 
\iffalse
Define the pair of candidate solutions
\begin{equation}
    \vx^{(1)}_k = \arg \underset{\vx \in \setX_k}{\max} \vv_{\textsf{priv}}^T \vx; \quad
    \vx^{(2)}_k = \arg \underset{\vx \in \setX_k}{\min} \vv_{\textsf{priv}}^T \vx 
\end{equation}
Note that $\vx^{(1)}_k$ and $\vx^{(2)}_k$ correspond to the support of the $k$-largest and $k$-smallest entries of $\vv_{\textsf{priv}}$ respectively. Between these two candidates, the one which attains the larger objective value corresponds to the solution of \eqref{eq:noisy_dks}; i.e., we have
\begin{equation}
    \hat{\vx}_k = \arg \underset{i \in \{1,2\}}{\max} |\vv_{\textsf{priv}}^T \vx_k^{(i)}|.
\end{equation}
\fi
Following the line of argument in \cite{papailiopoulos2014finding}, it suffices to examine the top-$k$ and bottom-$k$ support of $\vv_{\textsf{priv}}$, and then output the candidate that achieves a larger objective. Thus, the post-processing step remains computationally efficient, as it only requires examining an additional candidate solution compared to its non-private analogue \eqref{eq:non-private_baseline}. 

Since the post-processing property of DP guarantees that the privacy properties of $\vv_{\textsf{priv}}$ directly transfer to $\hat{\vx}_k$, the key question that remains to be answered is how exactly the private principal component $\vv_{\textsf{priv}}$ should be calculated - this is the main focus of this paper. To this end, we 
develop techniques that can provide meaningful privacy guarantees while ensuring that good utility of the overall procedure (computing the principal component privately followed by rank-$1$ approximation) can be attained. We now outline our main ideas for computing $\vv_{\textsf{priv}}$.

\noindent \textbf{A first attempt:} In order to compute the principal component of $\vA$ under edge-DP , a simple idea is to apply the technique of {\em output perturbation}. That is, we compute the global sensitivity of $\vv$ under edge-DP and add i.i.d. noise to $\vv$ that is calibrated to this sensitivity value. More specifically, we can compute the global $\ell_2$ sensitivity of $\vv$, and use it to instantiate the Gaussian mechanism in order to provide $(\epsilon,\delta)$ DP. The global $\ell_2$ sensitivity of $\vv$ under edge-DP can be bounded by the following claim (see \cite{gonem2018smooth}).
\begin{lemma}\label{lemma:GS}
The global $\ell_2$ sensitivity of $\vv$ is at most  $\sqrt{2}$ under edge-DP. 
\end{lemma}
\iffalse
\begin{proof}
Let $\vA$ and $\vA'$ be the respective adjacency matrices of a pair of neighboring graphs $\setG$ and $\setG'$. Since both $\vA$ and $\vA'$ are non-negative, their respective principal components $\vv$ and $\vv'$ are also non-negative. The maximum possible $\ell_2$ distance $\|\vv-\vv'\|_2$ between a such a pair of non-negative principal components is $\sqrt{2}$. The claimed bound on the global sensitivity then follows.
\end{proof}
\fi

%\anil{can we translate this to error for the DkS problem itself?}
Hence, using the above global sensitivity estimate of $\vv$, we can apply the standard Gaussian mechanism to privatize $\vv$. However, as we have already seen, the issue with this approach is that the global sensitivity estimate is large, which in turn requires injecting a large amount of noise to achieve a target privacy requirement. Consequently, this can result in a severe degradation in utility.

\noindent \textbf{Towards a second attempt: Analyzing local sensitivity.}
In general, smaller sensitivity values are preferable as they require injecting smaller amounts of noise to achieve a desired privacy guarantee, thereby offering better utility. This begs the following question: {\em is there room for improvement?} 
Providing an answer requires analyzing how close the local $\ell_2$ sensitivity of the principal components of real graphs are to the global sensitivity estimate of Lemma \ref{lemma:GS}. 
Although adding noise calibrated to local sensitivity is not guaranteed to be privacy preserving \cite{nissim2007smooth}, estimating the local sensitivity can still prove useful as it signifies the minimum amount of noise that is necessary to be added to the principal component. Hence, if we observe that the local $\ell_2$ sensitivity of $\vv$ is much smaller than $\sqrt{2}$ on a given graph, we can employ {\em instance-specific} noise addition schemes \cite{nissim2007smooth,dwork2009differential}  which offer privacy while injecting smaller amounts of noise compared to calibrating noise to the global sensitivity. As a consequence, we can obtain much better utility compared to the global sensitivity based Gaussian mechanism. 

 In order to realize the above objective, first we must estimate the local $\ell_2$ sensitivity of $\vv$ under edge-DP. We demonstrate that under a mild requirement on the spectral gap of $\setG$, the following bound on the local sensitivity can be derived.

\begin{theorem}\label{theo:LS}
If $\textsf{GAP}(\setG) > \sqrt{2}/(\sqrt{2}-1)$, then the local $\ell_2$ sensitivity of $\vv$ under edge-DP is at most 
\begin{equation}
   \textsf{LS}_{\vv}(\setG) \leq \frac{2\sqrt{v_{\pi(1)}^2 + v_{\pi(2)}^2}}{\textsf{GAP}(\mathcal{G})},
\end{equation}
where $v_{\pi(1)}$ and $v_{\pi(2)}$ denote the largest and second-largest entries of $\vv$ respectively.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Please refer to \autoref{appendix:local_sens}. 
\end{proof}

Note that the numerator term in the above upper bound is at most $2\sqrt{2}\|\vv\|_{\infty}$. We conclude that for graphs whose eigen-gap $\textsf{GAP}(\setG)$ exceeds $\sqrt{2}/(\sqrt{2}-1)$, the local $\ell_2$ sensitivity of the principal component $\vv$ is small when the entries of $\vv$ are ``spread out'' in magnitude (i.e., $\|\vv\|_{\infty}$ is small ) and the eigen-gap is large. At this point, it is instructive to compare the bound of Theorem \ref{theo:LS} with its counterpart of \cite{gonem2018smooth}. In particular, \cite{gonem2018smooth} establishes that under a more general condition than edge-DP\footnote{The notion of privacy considered in \cite{gonem2018smooth} concerns a pair of neighboring datasets $\vA$ and $\vA'$ which differ by a unit spectral norm perturbation; i.e., $\vA' = \vA + \vE$, where $\|\vE\|_2\leq 1$. Note that edge-DP implies that a pair of of neighboring adjacency matrices exhibit a unit spectral norm perturbation, but the converse is not true.}, the local sensitivity of the principal component is at most 
$\max\{3C/\textsf{GAP}(\setG),\sqrt{2}\}$,
where $C > 0$ is an unspecified, universal constant. While this result also captures the fact that matrices with large eigen-gaps exhibit low $\ell_2$ sensitivity, the dependence on the numerator term is unclear since $C$ is not explicitly specified. This poses a problem as the value of the local sensitivity estimate cannot be calculated on real datasets. 
In contrast, for real graphs which satisfy the eigen-gap condition $\textsf{GAP}(\setG) > \sqrt{2}/(\sqrt{2}-1)$, our local sensitivity estimate can be readily computed. 
The value of this estimate for various real-world graph datasets is presented in \autoref{tab:local_sensitivity}, and contrasted with the global sensitivity upper bound of $\sqrt{2}$. It is evident that the local sensitivity of $\vv$ on real graphs can be at least $2$ orders of magnitude smaller compared to the global sensitivity estimate of $\sqrt{2}$. This motivates the application of instance-specific noise-addition mechanisms for providing DP, the first of which we consider is the smooth sensitivity framework of \cite{nissim2007smooth}.

