%The main difficulty in implementing this framework lies in computing the $\ell_1$ sensitivity-$1$ statistic $\gamma(\mathcal{G})$ in step 2, as solving the optimization problem \eqref{eq:P1} for a given parameter $\beta$ is a difficult proposition in general. 
In a recent breakthrough, it was shown in \cite{li2024computing} that the PTR framework can still be successfully applied if $\gamma(\mathcal{G})$ is replaced by any non-negative, sensitivity-$1$ statistic $\phi(\mathcal{G})$ that is a global lower bound on $\gamma(\mathcal{G})$; i.e., we have 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:surrogate}
    \gamma(\mathcal{G}) \geq \phi(\mathcal{G}) \geq 0, \forall \; \setG.
\end{equation}
This results in the following modified PTR framework; first introduced in \cite{li2024computing}.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Propose an upper bound \( \beta \) on the local $\ell_2$ sensitivity $\textsf{LS}_{\vv}(\setG)$.
    \item Compute lower bound $\phi(\setG)$ on the distance $\gamma(\setG)$ defined in problem \eqref{eq:P1}.   
    \item Release \( \hat{\phi}(\mathcal{G}) := \phi(\mathcal{G}) + \text{Lap}\left(\frac{1}{\epsilon_1}\right) \).
    \item If \( \hat{\phi}(\mathcal{G}) \leq \frac{\ln (1 / \delta)}{\epsilon_1} \),
    return no response.
    \item If \( \hat{\phi}(G) \geq \frac{\ln(1 / \delta)}{\epsilon_1} \),
    return \( \mathbf{v} + \setN\left(0, \sigma^2\cdot \mathbf{I}_n\right) \), where $\sigma^2 = 2\beta^2 \log(2 / \delta)/{\epsilon_2^2}$
\end{enumerate}


The upshot is that {\em if} we can find a suitable surrogate function $\phi(\cdot)$ which satisfies \eqref{eq:surrogate}, and is simpler to compute compared to solving \eqref{eq:P1} for $\gamma(\cdot)$, then we can apply the framework successfully. However, the work of \cite{li2024computing} does not provide a general recipe for computing such requisite surrogates. Hence, a major contribution of our work lies in constructing a suitable $\phi(\cdot)$ for the  given problem. The main ideas underpinning our approach are explained next. 

\noindent {\bf $\bullet$ Construction of $\phi(\cdot)$:} The key idea is the following result which can be informally stated as follows: if the eigen-gap of $\setG$ is not ``too small'' and the distance between a candidate graph $\setG'$ and $\setG$ is not ``too large'' then, the local $\ell_2$ sensitivity of $\vv$ on $\mathcal{G}'$
can be appropriately upper bounded in ``simple'' terms. Formally, we have the following claim. 

\begin{theorem}\label{theo:upp_bound}
     Assume that the following two conditions hold.
     \begin{enumerate}
         \item[{\bf (A1:)}] $\textsf{GAP}(\mathcal{G}) > {2}/(\sqrt{2}-1)$, and
         \item[{\bf (A2:)}] $d(\mathcal{G},\mathcal{G}') < (1-1/\sqrt{2})\cdot\textsf{GAP}(\mathcal{G})$.
     \end{enumerate}
     Then, the local $\ell_2$ sensitivity of $\vv$ on $\setG'$ can be upper bounded by
    \begin{equation}
        \textsf{LS}_{\vv}(\mathcal{G}') \leq \frac{2}{\textsf{GAP}(\mathcal{G})-d(\mathcal{G},\mathcal{G}') } \cdot
        \biggl[\frac{2d(\mathcal{G},\mathcal{G}')}{\textsf{GAP}(\mathcal{G})}+ \sqrt{v_{{\pi(1)}}^2+v_{{\pi(2)}}^2}\biggr].
    \end{equation}
\end{theorem}


\noindent\begin{proof}
Please refer to \autoref{appendix:main_theorem}.  
\end{proof}

\noindent We will utilize the above result as follows. Define  
$$\theta(\mathcal{G}'):= \frac{2}{\textsf{GAP}(\mathcal{G})-d(\mathcal{G},\mathcal{G}') } \cdot
    \biggl[\frac{2d(\mathcal{G},\mathcal{G}')}{\textsf{GAP}(\mathcal{G})}+ \sqrt{v_{{\pi(1)}}^2+v_{{\pi(2)}}^2}\biggr].$$
Consequently, we can consider replacing $\textsf{LS}_{\vv}(\mathcal{G}')$ in \eqref{eq:P1} with the upper bound $\theta(\mathcal{G}')$.
Doing so yields the problem
\begin{equation}\label{eq:P2}
    \begin{aligned}
     \phi(\mathcal{G}) := \min ~ & d(\mathcal{G},\mathcal{G}') \\
        \textrm{s.t.} ~ & \theta(\mathcal{G}') \geq \beta
    \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
Since the feasible set of \eqref{eq:P2} is a superset of that of \eqref{eq:P1}, it follows that \eqref{eq:P2} is a relaxation of \eqref{eq:P1}. Hence, the solution of the above problem yields a lower bound on $\gamma(\mathcal{G})$, as desired. Furthermore, by design, $\phi(\mathcal{G})$ is non-negative and has $\ell_1$ sensitivity equal to $1$. Hence, $\phi(\mathcal{G})$ is a suitable candidate for implementing the PTR framework.



\noindent {\bf $\bullet$ Computing $\phi(.)$:} The tractability of the PTR framework hinges on our ability to solve problem \eqref{eq:P2} in an efficient manner in order to compute $\phi(.)$. By construction, the problem asks to find the smallest value of $d(\mathcal{G},\mathcal{G}')$ such that the inequality 
\begin{equation}\label{eq:P3}
    \frac{2}{\textsf{GAP}(\mathcal{G})-d(\mathcal{G},\mathcal{G}') } \cdot
    \biggl[\frac{2d(\mathcal{G},\mathcal{G}')}{\textsf{GAP}(\mathcal{G})}+ \sqrt{v_{{\pi(1)}}^2+v_{{\pi(2)}}^2}\biggr]
    \geq \beta
\end{equation}
is valid. Observe that for a given value of $\beta$, all the involved parameters can be readily computed from $\mathcal{G}$. Furthermore, the fact that $\theta(\mathcal{G}')$ (i.e., the LHS of the above inequality) is monotonically increasing with $d(\mathcal{G},\mathcal{G}')$, facilitates simple solution. In fact, for a given $\beta$, the statistic $\phi(.)$ can be computed in {\em closed form} according to the formula

\begin{equation}\label{eq:phi}
    \phi(\mathcal{G}) = 
    \left\lceil \frac{\beta \cdot \textsf{GAP}^2(\setG) - 2 \textsf{GAP}(
    \setG)\sqrt{v_{\pi(1)}^2 + v_{\pi(2)}^2 }}{4 + \beta \cdot \textsf{GAP}(\mathcal{G})} \right\rceil.
\end{equation}

\noindent {\bf $\bullet$ Valid choices of $\beta$:} Selecting the parameter $\beta$ is a key component of implementing PTR. First, we specify an interval of values of $\beta$ which will be considered. We start from verifying the following condition; namely, whether for a given value of $\beta$, the solution of problem \eqref{eq:P3} satisfies $\phi(\mathcal{G}) < (1-1/\sqrt{2})\cdot\textsf{GAP}(\mathcal{G})$, as this assumption is required in \autoref{theo:upp_bound} to establish that $\theta(\mathcal{G}')$ is a valid upper bound on $ \textsf{LS}(\mathcal{G}')$. As we show next, ensuring that this condition is met translates into a maximum allowable value of $\beta$.

Since $\theta(\mathcal{G})$ is monotonically increasing with $d(\mathcal{G},\mathcal{G}')$, the largest value of $\beta$ which can be satisfied occurs when $d(\mathcal{G},\mathcal{G}') = (1-1/\sqrt{2})\cdot\textsf{GAP}(\mathcal{G}) - 1$. From \eqref{eq:P3}, this corresponds to the following upper bound on $\beta$.
\begin{equation}
    \beta_u:= \frac{2\sqrt{2}}{\textsf{GAP}(\mathcal{G})} 
    \biggl[ 2-\sqrt{2} + \sqrt{v_{\pi(1)}^2+v_{\pi(2)}^2} \biggr] 
\end{equation}
By the same principle, the largest value of $\beta$ for which $d(\mathcal{G},\mathcal{G}') = 0$ in problem \eqref{eq:P3} occurs for
\begin{equation}
    \beta_l := \frac{2\sqrt{v_{\pi(1)}^2+v_{\pi(2)}^2}}{\textsf{GAP}(\mathcal{G})},
\end{equation}
which corresponds to the upper bound on the local $\ell_2$ sensitivity of $\vv$ on $\setG$. 
Hence, for any choice of $\beta \in (\beta_l,\beta_u)$, the statistic $\phi(.)$ can be computed according to \eqref{eq:phi}. 

\noindent {\bf $\bullet$ Privacy guarantees:} Here, we establish that if the PTR algorithm is implemented with $\beta \in (\beta_l,\beta_u)$, then its output exhibits the following DP guarantees. 

\begin{theorem}\label{ptr_dp}
For $\beta \in (\beta_l,\beta_u)$, the PTR algorithm is $(\epsilon_1 + \epsilon_2,\delta)$ differentially-private.   
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Please refer to \autoref{proof_dp}.
\end{proof}

\noindent {\bf $\bullet$ Policy for selecting $\beta:$} We finally outline our policy for selecting a suitable $\beta \in (\beta_l,\beta_u)$. Note that for all $\beta > \beta_l$, we have $\phi(\setG) > 0$. Furthermore, from \eqref{eq:phi}, it is evident that $\phi(\setG)$ increases monotonically with $\beta$. Hence, larger values of $\beta \in (\beta_l,\beta_u)$ correspond to higher likelihood of the noisy statistic $\hat{\phi(\setG)}$ exceeding the threshold $\ln(1/\delta)/\epsilon_1$ (for a fixed set of privacy parameters $(\epsilon,\delta)$), which in turn increases the odds of the PTR algorithm yielding a response. However, larger values of $\beta$ also result in increased noise injection in the release step of PTR. Hence, a suitable $\beta$ should strike the ``sweet spot'' between minimizing noise injection and maximizing the odds of a successful response. Next, we demonstrate how the choice of $\beta$ impacts these two conflicting objectives. 

For a given $\beta \in (\beta_l,\beta_u)$, we say that the PTR algorithm {\em succeeds} if the value of the noisy statistic $\hat{\phi}(\setG)$ exceeds the threshold $\ln(1/\delta)/\epsilon_1$, which implies that the algorithm responds with an output according to step 5. Hence, the {\em success probability} of PTR corresponds to the event ${\sf Prob}(\hat{\phi}(\setG) \geq \log(1/\delta)/\epsilon_1)$, where the randomness is w.r.t. the Laplace random variable ${\sf Lap}(0,1/\epsilon_1)$. 
We now reveal how the success probability is affected by the choice of $\beta$. To this end, it will be convenient to express $\phi$ (as defined in \eqref{eq:phi}) as 
\begin{equation}
    \phi(\setG) = \left \lceil \tau(\setG)\right\rceil,
\end{equation}
where $\tau(\setG)$ is the fraction in \eqref{eq:phi}. Note that by design, we have 
$$ \left \lfloor \tau(\setG) \right\rfloor + 1\geq \phi(\setG) \geq \tau(\setG).$$ 
Since $\tau(.)$ increases monotonically with $\beta$, increasing $\tau(.)$ also increases $\phi(.)$, which in turn, directly influences the outcome of the threshold test. 
Next, we demonstrate that controlling the value of $\tau(.)$ through $\beta$ is sufficient to derive lower bounds on the success probability of PTR.

\begin{theorem}\label{ptr_success}
If $\beta$ is selected to satisfy
\begin{equation}\label{eq:threshold}
\tau(\setG) = p\cdot \frac{\log(1/\delta)}{\epsilon_1}, \forall \; p \in (1,2],
\end{equation}
then the success probability of PTR is at least $1-\frac{\delta^{(p-1)}}{2}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Please refer to \autoref{proof_success}. 
\end{proof}
\noindent Intuitively, the smaller the value of $p$, the closer the value of $\phi(.)$ is to the threshold $\log(1/\delta)/\epsilon_1$, and hence the lower the probability of success. This is captured by the above theorem, which shows that as $p$ decreases, the success probability diminishes at the rate $1-O(\delta^{(p-1)})$. As $p \rightarrow 1$, the lower bound on the success probability shrinks to $1/2$.

The value of $\beta$ which satisfies  \eqref{eq:threshold} is given by
\begin{equation}
    \label{eq:ptr_beta}
    \beta = \frac{2}{\textsf{GAP}(\setG)} \cdot \biggl[\frac{2p\log(1/\delta)/\epsilon_1 + \textsf{GAP}(\setG)\sqrt{v_{\pi(1)}^2 + v_{\pi(2)}^2 }}{\textsf{GAP}(\setG) - p\log(1/\delta)/\epsilon_1}\biggr].
\end{equation}
We can obtain several interesting insights from the above formula. First, fixing all parameters in \eqref{eq:ptr_beta} except $p$, we see that $\beta$ diminishes as $p$ is reduced. We conclude that smaller values of $p$ result in the injection of smaller amounts of noise, but this comes at the expense of reduced odds of the algorithm succeeding. Hence, the parameter $p$ directly controls the trade-off between noise-injection levels and the success probability. Second, fixing all parameters except $\textsf{GAP}(\setG)$, we observe that $\beta = O(1/\textsf{GAP}(\setG))$, which implies that graphs with a large spectral gap are amenable to smaller levels of noise injection, for fixed privacy parameters and a fixed success probability. Finally, fixing all parameters except $(\epsilon_1,\delta)$, we note that smaller privacy parameters (i.e., larger values of $\log(1/\delta)/\epsilon_1$) mandate larger values of $\beta$. Hence, $\beta$ can be viewed as a quantity that depends on the privacy parameters. In fact, for $\beta$ to be valid (i.e., $\beta >0$), we obtain the necessary condition
\begin{equation}
    \log(1/\delta)/\epsilon_1 < \textsf{GAP}(\setG)/p,
\end{equation}
which reveals that the term $\textsf{GAP}(\setG)/p$ affects the privacy budget. Fixing $p$, graphs with larger eigen-gaps can thus accommodate smaller privacy parameter settings. On the other hand, for a fixed graph, reducing $p$ can
allow selecting smaller privacy parameters, albeit at the expense of lower odds of the algorithm yielding a response. 
We conclude with a final sanity check to ensure that $\beta$ computed according to \eqref{eq:ptr_beta} lies in the interval $(\beta_l,\beta_u)$. 
\begin{proposition}\label{ptr_parameters}
For a fixed graph with spectral gap $\textsf{GAP}(\setG)$, and parameters $\epsilon_1,\delta,p$, the condition
$$\frac{\log(1/\delta)}{\epsilon_1} < (1-1/\sqrt{2})\frac{\textsf{GAP}(\setG) }{p} \implies \beta \in (\beta_l,\beta_u).  $$
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Please refer to \autoref{proof_parameters}.
\end{proof}

