\begin{theorem}
  \label{ptr_performance} 
  Let $p \in (1,2]$. Consider symmetric adjacency matrix $\mathbf{A}\in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$ with principal component $\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\sigma_1 \geq \cdots \geq \sigma_d \geq 0$ denoting its singular values ($\sigma_i = |\lambda_i| \quad i \in \{1, 2, \dots, d\}$. Compute function  $\beta$ based on \eqref{ptr_beta}. Then Algorithm  ~\ref{alg:ptr} with noise variance $\sigma_{ptr} =\epsilon^{-1}\sqrt{2\beta \log{(2/\delta)}} $,  with probability $1- \delta^{(p-1)}/2$, yields private DKS with edge density: 
  \begin{equation}
    \mathbb{E}[d_k^{ptr}] \geq d_k^*-\frac{1}{k-1}\left(2\left|\lambda_2\right|+2 \sqrt{n} \lambda_1 \sigma_{ptr}+(n-1) \lambda_1 \sigma_{ptr}^2\right).\label{ptr_edge_density}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}The complete proof of this theorem can be found in Appendix~\ref{proof_ptr}.
\fi 
\section{The Private Power Method}

In this section, we consider an alternative approach for computing private principal components, based on the Private Power Method (PPM) of \cite{hardt2014noisy,hardt2013beyond}.
Our motivation for considering such an approach is based on the fact that PTR requires a condition on the eigen-gap of $\setG$ in order to be successfully applied. Furthermore, when PTR successfully responds, it outputs a pair of private quantities $\hat{\phi}(\setG)$ and the noisy principal component, which costs more in terms of the overall privacy budget. In contrast, PPM requires ${\sf GAP}(\setG) > 0$, but does not specify a minimum threshold on the value of the eigen-gap itself. Furthermore, its output set is a singleton - a noisy principal component satisfying $(\epsilon,\delta)$-DP, which can reflect a smaller privacy budget compared to PTR.
In essence, PPM is a ``noisy'' variant of the classic (non-private) power method which computes the principal component of a matrix in an iterative fashion. Executing each step entails performing a matrix-vector multiplication, followed by normalization. In \cite{hardt2014noisy,hardt2013beyond}, this algorithm is made DP by adding Gaussian noise to each matrix-vector multiplication, followed by normalization. By calibrating the variance of the Gaussian noise added in each iteration to the $\ell_2$-sensitivity of matrix-vector multiplication, the final iterate of PPM (after a pre-determined number of iterations have been carried out) can be shown to satisfy $(\epsilon,\delta)$-DP. The notion of privacy considered defines a pair of matrices $\vA$ and $\vA'$ to be neighboring if they differ in one entry by at-most $1$. As per the authors of \cite{hardt2014noisy}, this is most meaningful when the entries of the data matrix $\vA$ lie in $[0,1]$. Note that when $\vA$ and $\vA'$ correspond to a pair of neighboring adjacency matrices, this naturally coincides with the notion of edge-DP for undirected graphs. The complete algorithm is described in Algorithm~\ref{alg:algo1}.

\begin{algorithm}  % Use [H] to force placement here
    \DontPrintSemicolon 
    \SetAlgoLined
    \footnotesize
    \textbf{Input:} Symmetric $A \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n}$, no. of iterations $L$, privacy parameters $\epsilon$, $\delta > 0$.\;
    \textbf{Output:} Private vertex set $\mathcal{S}_\text{ppm}$.\;
    \textbf{Initialize:} Let $\mathbf{v}_0$ be a random unit direction and set $\sigma=\epsilon^{-1}\sqrt{4L \log{(1/\delta)}}$.\;
    \For{\( \ell = 1 \) \textbf{to} \( L \)}{
        Generate \( \mathbf{g}_{\ell} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \|\mathbf{v}_{\ell-1}\|_{\infty}^2 \sigma^2) \in \mathbb{R}^{n} \)\;
        Compute \( \mathbf{w}_{\ell} = A \mathbf{v}_{\ell-1} + \mathbf{g}_{\ell} \)\;
        Normalize $\vv_\ell = \vw_\ell/\|\vw_\ell\|_2$
    }
    Compute $\mathcal{S}_\text{PPM} =  \operatorname*{arg\,max}_{\mathbf{x} \in \mathcal{X}_k} |\mathbf{v}_L^T \mathbf{x}|$.\;
    \textbf{Return:} $\mathcal{S}_\text{PPM} $
    \caption{DP-D$k$S via PPM $(\mathbf{A},L,\epsilon,\delta)$}
    \label{alg:algo1}
\end{algorithm}
\vspace{+\baselineskip} % Adjust spacing here
The result of \cite{hardt2014noisy} provides the following guarantee regarding the output $\vv_L$ of the private power method (PPM).
\begin{proposition}\label{lem:ppi_differential_privacy}
   PPM satisfies $(\epsilon,\delta)$-DP and after $L=O(\frac{\lambda_1\log n}{\textsf{GAP}(\setG)})$ iterations, we have with probability at least $9/10$ that
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\|(\vI-\vv_{L}\vv_{L}^T)\vv\|_2 &\leq O\biggl(\frac{\sigma \cdot \max_{\ell \in [L]}\|\vv_{L}\|_{\infty}\cdot\sqrt{n\log L}}{\textsf{GAP}(\setG)}\biggr)
\end{split}
\end{equation} 
\end{proposition}
In terms of computational complexity, each step of PPM requires computing a sparse matrix-vector multiplication, which incurs $O(m)$ complexity, followed by noise addition and re-normalization, which incurs an additional $O(n)$ time. Hence, each step has complexity order $O(n+m)$. After $L$ iterations, the overall complexity is $O((n+m)L)$. If $L$ is chosen according to Proposition \ref{lem:ppi_differential_privacy}, this results in complexity of order $O((\lambda_1(n+m)\log n)/\textsf{GAP}(\setG))$.

