\section{Experimental Results}

In this section, we test the efficacy of the proposed DP algorithms on real-world graph datasets in terms of their privacy-utility trade-off and their runtime. 

\noindent $\bullet$ {\bf Datasets:}
Table ~\ref{tab:stats} provides a summary of the datasets, which were sourced from standard repositories ~\cite{kunegis2013konect,jure2014snap,nr}. %The selected datasets span diverse domains and scales, to highlight the inclusiveness and scalability of the proposed approaches.  
We pre-processed these datasets as follows: if the original network was directed, we symmetrized the arcs, removed all self-loops, and extracted the largest connected component. Note that the datasets are all unweighted.

\noindent $\bullet$ {\bf Implementation:}
We performed all our experiments in Python on a Linux work-station equipped with 132GB RAM and an Intel i7 processor. We used the following settings for PPM and PTR.

\noindent $(1)$ {\bf PTR:} We set the privacy budgets $(\epsilon_1=\epsilon_2=\epsilon = 3)$ for noise addition in both the Laplace and Gaussian mechanisms. Additionally, the parameter $\delta$ was set to be $\delta = 1/m$, where $m$ is the number of edges in each network. We set the lower bound on the success probability of obtaining a response  in \autoref{alg:ptr} to be $0.95$, and then, based on \autoref{ptr_success}, set $p = 1 - (1/\log_{10}(\delta))$. 

\noindent $(2)$ {\bf PPM:} We set $(\epsilon = 3)$ and the parameter $\delta$ was fixed to be $(\delta = 10^{-12})$ across all datasets. An open question with practical implementation of PPM is how to select the number of iterations $L$, which has to be specified beforehand. The main issue is that \cite{hardt2014noisy} only provides the order of iterations required to achieve a certain utility bound, which scales like $O(\frac{\lambda_1\log n}{\textsf{GAP}(\setG)}))$. We experimentally observed that simply setting $L = \frac{\lambda_1\log n}{\textsf{GAP}(\setG)}$ provides good empirical performance across the different datasets. Performing more iterations typically degraded the performance of the algorithm, in addition to also increasing the run-time complexity. 

Further results for a more detailed investigation of the proposed solutions across different values of the privacy parameters can be found in Appendix \ref{additional_result}. As per the conference's submission guidelines for reproducibility, we will open source our code upon the acceptance of this paper.

\noindent $\bullet$ {\bf Results:}
The empirical performance of Algorithm~\ref{alg:ptr} and Algorithm~\ref{alg:algo1} across different real-world graph datasets is depicted in Figure~\ref{fig:density}.  Each sub-figure plots the edge-density versus size curve obtained using the two different algorithms. As a baseline, we consider the non-private algorithm for approximate D$k$S using principal components, as described in \autoref{non_private_dks}. In addition, we also include an upper bound for the edge density of the D$k$S based on Proposition~\autoref{upper_bound}.
To generate each plot, we executed Algorithm~\ref{alg:ptr} and and Algorithm~\ref{alg:algo1} for 100 different realizations, with each private principal component used to generate a different edge density-size curve.
For each subgraph size $k$, we depict the average edge density attained by each method across all realizations, within one standard deviation (vertical lines). Meanwhile, the average run-time complexity of PTR and PPM (across all realizations) is shown in Table \ref{tab:Scalability}. Our main findings can be summarized as follows.

