From \autoref{fig:density}, it is evident that both PTR and PPM output subgraphs whose edge-density closely matches that of the non-private solution, demonstrating their effectiveness and practical utility. This occurs for a modest privacy budget of $\epsilon \geq 3$. In addition, both solutions demonstrate small variance around the average value of the edge density indicating both consistency and reliability.

\noindent {\bf (2):}
In general, both PTR and PPM both exhibit comparable accuracy in terms of edge density. However, PTR incurs a larger overall privacy budget compared to PPM (twice more in terms of the $\epsilon$ parameter) to attain the same performance. This can be attributed to the fact that PTR outputs two parameters privately: the noisy principal component and the noisy statistic $\hat{\setG}$, whereas PPM only outputs the former. 

