\section{Conclusions}

In this paper, we propose two algorithms for the densest-$k$-subgraph (D$k$S) problem under edge differential privacy (DP). We build them on the non-private algorithm of \cite{papailiopoulos2014finding}, which uses the principal component of the graph adjacency matrix. The first approach is based on computing principal components via output perturbation. The large gap between the local and the global sensitivity of principal components under edge DP, motivated us to use instance-specific sensitivity mechanisms like smooth sensitivity. Next, we show that applying prior bounds on smooth sensitivity can produce values very close to global sensitivity. This leads us to develop a new practical and powerful PTR framework which obviates the computational complexity issues inherent in the standard framework, while facilitating simple selection of the algorithm parameters based on the allocated privacy budget and the spectral gap. The second approach is based on the private power method, which is a noisy variant of the classic non-private power method that can be used to compute an approximate principal component under DP. 
%we develop a novel solution within the propose-test-release (PTR) framework to significantly lighten the required noise level. Specifically, we introduce DP-D$K$S using PTR, demonstrating a thorough understanding of how to adjust function sensitivity based on privacy parameters and the graph spectrum. 
%We also employ the private power method as another solution for DP-D$K$S, representing a theoretical upper bound for its utility performance. 
We test both approaches on real-world graphs, and demonstrate that they can both attain performance comparable to the non-private solution while adhering to a modest privacy budget. In our experiments, while PTR requires a larger privacy budget, it achieves its performance with a $180$ fold runtime improvement over PPM.

